var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Julia","text":"A plan holds everything that depends only on the nodes and the array size: the chosen window, the binned nodes and the FFT plans. Creating it costs about as much as a few transforms; afterwards every transform with these nodes is fast.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#A-first-transform","page":"Julia","text":"Nodes are given as a d  J matrix with entries in -12 12); in one dimension a vector works as well. The second argument is the size of the equidistant array.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nJ, N = 2000, (64, 48)\nk = rand(2, J) .- 0.5\np = plan_nfft(k, N)\nNFFTPlan{Float64} of size (64, 48) along dims 1:2 at 2000 nodes: ExpSemicircle window, W = 10, σ = 1.65, predicted error 9.124339286200806e-9\n\nThe plan behaves like the J  bmN matrix of the NDFT:\n\nsize(p)\n(2000, 3072)\n\nApplying it to an array of size N gives the values at the nodes, the adjoint maps back:\n\nf = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nfHat = p * f\ng = adjoint(p) * fHat\nsize(fHat), size(g)\n((2000,), (64, 48))","title":"A first transform"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#How-accurate-is-it?","page":"Julia","text":"Without further options the plan aims at a relative error of 10^-8. accuracy(p) is the error the plan predicts for itself. We compare with the direct evaluation (NDFT), which NFFT.jl offers as a plan of its own:\n\npd = NDFTPlan(k, N)\naccuracy(p), norm(p * f - pd * f) / norm(pd * f)\n(9.124339286200806e-9, 5.525744276248731e-9)\n\nA different tolerance is a keyword away. The plan then chooses a narrower or wider window:\n\np4 = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-4)\np12 = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-12)\n[norm(q * f - pd * f) / norm(pd * f) for q in (p4, p12)]\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5.5228847274139656e-5\n 4.814886236052271e-13","title":"How accurate is it?"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Avoiding-allocations","page":"Julia","text":"p * f allocates its output. In loops, use mul! with preallocated arrays:\n\nmul!(fHat, p, f)\nmul!(g, adjoint(p), fHat);","title":"Avoiding allocations"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Single-precision","page":"Julia","text":"eltype selects the precision of the data; the default follows the nodes. In single precision the positions of the nodes on the grid are still computed in double precision, so large grids do not lose accuracy.\n\np32 = plan_nfft(k, N; eltype=Float32, reltol=1e-5)\nfHat32 = p32 * ComplexF32.(f)\neltype(fHat32), norm(fHat32 - pd * f) / norm(pd * f)\n(ComplexF32, 2.5873535245237865e-6)","title":"Single precision"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Threads","page":"Julia","text":"All stages run multi-threaded with Threads.nthreads() threads (start Julia with julia -t 8 or -t auto). nthreads limits the number for one plan. On Apple Silicon use the number of performance cores (-t auto counts the efficiency cores too), and on a machine busy with other work fewer threads than cores: a 1D transform runs its FFT in one piece per thread, and a piece on a slow or busy core holds up the whole transform.\n\np1 = plan_nfft(k, N; nthreads=1)\np1 * f ≈ p * f\ntrue\n\nOne plan must not be used by several tasks at the same time, since it owns its workspace. copy(p) gives a plan with its own workspace that shares the read-only parts:\n\nplans = [copy(p) for _ in 1:2]\nresults = Vector{Vector{ComplexF64}}(undef, 2)\nThreads.@threads for i in 1:2\n  results[i] = plans[i] * f\nend\nresults[1] == results[2]\ntrue","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#New-nodes","page":"Julia","text":"nodes! exchanges the nodes and keeps all other settings; the number of nodes may change.\n\nnodes!(p, rand(2, 3000) .- 0.5)\nsize(p * f)\n(3000,)","title":"New nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Without-a-plan","page":"Julia","text":"For a single transform, nfft and nfft_adjoint create the plan internally:\n\nfHat = nfft(k, f)\ng = nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat)\nsize(g)\n(64, 48)","title":"Without a plan"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Where-to-go-next","page":"Julia","text":"Transform only some dimensions, e.g. many coils or A-scans: Transforms along Dimensions.\nReal data, cosine and sine transforms, points that are non-equidistant on both sides, GPUs: the other how-to guides in the navigation.\nWhat the plan computes and why it is fast: Mathematical Background and How NFFT.jl Works.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/parameters/#Choosing-Parameters","page":"Choosing Parameters","text":"This guide shows how to override the automatic choice of window, width and oversampling, how to use a window of your own and how to let FFTW measure its plans. Usually reltol is all you need; how the plan turns it into parameters is explained in Accuracy and Parameters.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nN = (48, 48)\nk = rand(2, 4000) .- 0.5\nf = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nexact = NDFTPlan(k, N) * f\nrelerr(p) = norm(p * f - exact) / norm(exact)\nrelerr (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Choosing Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/parameters/#Restrict-the-window-family","page":"Choosing Parameters","text":"window = KaiserBessel() or ExpSemicircle() restricts the search to one family; the default :auto lets both compete:\n\npkb = plan_nfft(k, N; window=KaiserBessel(), reltol=1e-8)\npes = plan_nfft(k, N; window=ExpSemicircle(), reltol=1e-8)\nNFFT.parameters(pkb), NFFT.parameters(pes)\n((window = KaiserBessel(), W = 10, σ = 1.75), (window = ExpSemicircle(), W = 9, σ = 2.05))","title":"Restrict the window family"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/parameters/#Set-the-width-and-the-oversampling-factor","page":"Choosing Parameters","text":"W and σ restrict the search as well; with both given, only the window shape comes from the table. W may be odd (see Mathematical Background for W = 2m). A σ between the tabulated values (steps of 0.05) uses the shape of the nearest one, and accuracy(p) is then an estimate.\n\np = plan_nfft(k, N; W=7, σ=1.5)\nNFFT.parameters(p), relerr(p)\n((window = ExpSemicircle(), W = 7, σ = 1.5), 6.1308099397503945e-6)","title":"Set the width and the oversampling factor"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/parameters/#Use-a-window-of-your-own","page":"Choosing Parameters","text":"Any other window can be used through CustomWindow. The function receives the offset z in grid points and the width W; it must be symmetric and vanish for z ge W2. Without a table, W and σ must be given and accuracy(p) is unknown (NaN):\n\ngauss = CustomWindow((z, W) -> abs(z) < W / 2 ? exp(-(2z / W)^2 * 9) : 0.0)\npg = plan_nfft(k, N; window=gauss, W=8, σ=2.0)\nrelerr(pg), accuracy(pg)\n(6.343713256861145e-5, NaN)","title":"Use a window of your own"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/parameters/#Let-FFTW-measure-its-plans","page":"Choosing Parameters","text":"The FFT on the oversampled grid uses FFTW. fft = FFTWBackend(flags=FFTW.MEASURE) lets FFTW measure its plans, which takes longer when creating the NFFT plan:\n\nusing FFTW\np = plan_nfft(k, N; fft=FFTWBackend(flags=FFTW.MEASURE))\n\nThe FFT itself is pruned to the lines that are not zero; see How NFFT.jl Works.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Let FFTW measure its plans"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#How-NFFT.jl-Works","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"This page explains what happens inside a plan and why it is fast. It is meant for readers who want to understand the implementation, compare it with other libraries or contribute; for using NFFT.jl, none of it is needed. The mathematics behind the three stages is in Mathematical Background.","title":"How NFFT.jl Works"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#What-a-plan-precomputes","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"Creating a plan does all work that depends only on the nodes and the sizes:\n\nParameters. From reltol the plan picks window family, width W and oversampling factor sigma with the lowest predicted cost (see Accuracy and Parameters).\nThe window as W polynomial pieces (below), and the correction factors 1hatvarphi for the kept modes.\nThe nodes, binned into tiles of the oversampled grid (below), with their coordinates in the order of the tiles.\nFFT plans for the passes of the pruned FFT, and the work arrays: the padded grid and one tile buffer per task.\n\nA transform then allocates no arrays, only a few kilobytes for scheduling its tasks. copy(p) shares the read-only parts and gets its own work arrays, so copies can run concurrently.","title":"What a plan precomputes"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#The-window-as-polynomial-pieces","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: Window evaluation)\n\nThe resampling needs W window values per node and dimension. Evaluating a Kaiser–Bessel or exponential-of-semicircle window directly costs a square root and an exponential or hyperbolic sine each. NFFT.jl splits the window into W pieces of one grid point width and fits each by a polynomial of degree W+3 in the offset s. All W support points of a node lie at the same offset within their pieces, so one s gives all W weights. The window is symmetric, which pairs the pieces: their even and odd parts come from one Horner chain in s^2, unrolled at compile time for each W. The weights cost a few nanoseconds per node and dimension, and the accuracy is that of the window itself.","title":"The window as polynomial pieces"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#The-grid-with-a-halo","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: Padded grid with a halo)\n\nThe oversampled grid is periodic: the support of a node near the edge continues on the other side. NFFT.jl stores the grid with a halo of W extra points per dimension. Before the resampling the halo is filled with copies from the opposite side, after the adjoint resampling it is added back. In between, every node reads or writes one block of W^D points without wrap-around, and the inner loops contain no modulo operations and no branches.\n\nThe node positions on the grid are always computed in double precision, also for single-precision data: in Float32, k tilde N would be off by about 10^-2 grid points for tilde N approx 4 cdot 10^5.","title":"The grid with a halo"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Resampling-on-tiles","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: Adjoint resampling with tile buffers)\n\nAt plan time the nodes are sorted into tiles of the grid by a parallel counting sort. A transform processes the grid tile by tile, and every task takes the next tile from a shared counter:\n\nResampling (grid to nodes): the task copies the tile with its halo into a private buffer and computes the values of the tile's nodes from it. Reading from the compact copy instead of the large grid makes the time independent of the grid size, which otherwise varies by up to 65 % through cache conflicts.\nAdjoint resampling (nodes to grid): the task adds the nodes of its tile into a zeroed private buffer of edge B + W + 1, which stays in the L1 or L2 cache, and then adds the buffer to the grid. Neighbouring buffers overlap, so this addition takes a lock for each tile of the grid it touches, one lock at a time. In 3D the buffer slides through a column of tiles and adds only the slices that the next tile no longer touches.\n\nEach node is handled exactly once, by one task, and no two tasks write to the same memory at the same time.","title":"Resampling on tiles"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#The-FFT","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: Pruned FFT)\n\nBefore the FFT of the direct transform, only the bmN kept modes in the corners of the grid are nonzero; after the FFT of the adjoint, only those are needed. NFFT.jl runs the FFT as one pass per dimension and skips the lines that contain only zeros (direct) or are not needed later (adjoint). In 2D with sigma = 2 the first pass of the direct transform touches half of the lines, in 3D the first two passes a quarter and a half; the adjoint saves the same in its last passes. Each pass is split into boxes of lines that run in parallel, each with a single-threaded batched FFTW plan: with ESTIMATE, FFTW's own threading produces plans up to 17 times slower than MEASURE plans, while single-threaded batched ESTIMATE plans come within 10–20 % of MEASURE. In 1D, FFTW's threading is used. The grid sizes are products of 2, 3, 5 and 7, not powers of two, so that sigma can be chosen freely.","title":"The FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Batch-strategies","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: Strategies for batch dimensions)\n\nWith dims, the data are viewed as (L_1 bmN L_3): L = L_1 L_3 slices, with the batch dimensions before and after the transformed ones (see Transforms along Dimensions for the usage). An NFFT plan chooses one of four strategies at plan time, in this order:\n\nSlices in parallel, if there are several threads and either many slices (L ge 4 per thread) or at least one small slice per thread (J + bmN le 2^15): every thread runs whole slices on its own single-threaded copy of the plan, which shares nodes, window and correction factors. Small transforms cannot use many threads; for 512 short 1D transforms this is about 50 times faster than running them one after the other.\nBatch kernels (1D and 2D, batch dimensions only before dims): the grid stores the L values of each grid point next to each other. The window weights of a node are evaluated once and applied to all L values with SIMD instructions.\nTransposition (1D and 2D, batch dimensions only after dims): the data are transposed into the layout of the batch kernels and back.\nA loop over the slices with the threaded transform (3D and higher, or batch dimensions on both sides of dims).\n\nRNFFT, NFCT and NFST plans run slices in parallel or loop over them. On a GPU, every kernel processes all slices in one launch.","title":"Batch strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Real-valued-transforms","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: RNFFT and NFCT/NFST)\n\nRNFFT. The values at the nodes are real exactly when the oversampled grid is real. The plan stores the half spectrum (n_1 ge 0), runs complex backward FFTs along dimensions 2 dots D on it and a complex-to-real FFT along dimension 1 into a real grid. The FFT and the adjoint resampling then cost about half of those of the complex NFFT.\nNFCT and NFST are NFFTs of the even and odd extension of the data on 2tildebm N points. Only the points 0 dots tilde N of each dimension are stored; the halo holds their mirror images, with a sign change for the NFST, so the resampling loops of the NFFT run unchanged. The FFT stage is a DCT-I (DST-I), computed as a real FFT of each extended line: FFTW's own DCT-I (REDFT00) needs mathcal O(n^2) operations for many sizes when planned with ESTIMATE, and planning it with MEASURE takes seconds to minutes.","title":"Real-valued transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Non-equidistant-on-both-sides","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: NNFFT)\n\nThe NNFFT spreads the sources with an outer window onto an equidistant grid, evaluates the Fourier series of that grid at the scaled targets with an ordinary NFFT, and divides by the Fourier transform of the outer window. Both node sets are first centred, which turns into phase factors. The spacing h = 1(2sigma_1 K) keeps the targets inside the band where the division is well conditioned; the grid is large enough that the spreading never wraps around. With Y_d and K_d the half-widths of sources and targets, the inner NFFT has about 4sigma_1 Y_d K_d modes per dimension (sigma_1 between 1.2 and 2.5): the cost follows the extent of the data, not the number of points. The error of the inner NFFT is amplified by at most A = hatvarphi(0)hatvarphi(1(2sigma_1)), so the plan chooses the outer window and the inner NFFT together, at the lowest predicted cost for the requested error.","title":"Non-equidistant on both sides"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#GPUs","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"(Image: GPU kernels)\n\nThe plan is set up on the CPU, with the same parameter choice and window polynomial; the nodes are binned on the CPU by the start of their support (below). The GPU holds, for each node, the offset of its support in the grid and its fractional position, in the working precision; the grid positions themselves were computed in double precision. Each stage is a KernelAbstractions.jl kernel:\n\nResampling, single precision, NFFT and RNFFT: by subproblems (below). A workgroup loads the tile of its bin from the periodic grid into local memory and resamples its nodes from there. Otherwise one work-item per node and transform reads from the padded grid; reads never conflict.\nAdjoint resampling, single precision: by subproblems. The nodes are binned by the start of their support; one workgroup takes up to 1024 nodes of one bin and adds them into the bin's tile, enlarged by the support width, in local memory. The work-items split the pairs of node and support point in all dimensions but the first and run along the first, contiguous one, so all of them are busy even at low node density. They add with integer atomics: the tile holds 32-bit fixed point numbers (scaled by 2^30 over the number of nodes times the subproblem's largest value), since Apple GPUs have no atomic floating point additions in local memory, and integer sums do not depend on the order. The tile is then added to the grid with atomic additions, for the NFFT and RNFFT directly into the periodic grid. The result agrees between calls up to rounding. Bins are smaller in 1D and 2D than local memory allows, so that several workgroups share a core.\nAdjoint resampling, double precision, 2D and 3D: one workgroup per tile of the grid, which loads the nodes of the 2^D bins that can reach the tile in chunks into local memory; for each node the work-items split the intersection of its support with the tile, and a barrier separates the nodes. The tile is written once, without atomic operations, and the result is the same in every call. In 1D, and for tiles that do not fit into local memory, the work-items add with atomic operations.\nHalo: filling and folding are gathers, again without atomics.\nIndex arithmetic inside the kernels is 32-bit, and decompositions by sizes known only at run time are unsigned; atomics are relaxed. On Apple GPUs 64-bit and signed divisions, and atomics with sequential consistency, each cost a factor of 2 to 10 in the kernels concerned.\nFFT: through AbstractFFTs, so the GPU package provides it (cuFFT, rocFFT, Metal).","title":"GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Where-the-ideas-come-from","page":"How NFFT.jl Works","text":"Tile buffers with locks and the sliding buffer in 3D, the even/odd evaluation of the window and the window family with a free exponent, optimised per W and sigma and stored as a table, follow ducc (M. Reinecke; Arras et al., A&A 646, A58, 2021). The table of NFFT.jl is computed by its own optimiser.\nThe exponential of semicircle window and the formulation of the type 3 transform follow FINUFFT (Barnett, Magland, af Klinteberg, SISC 41, 2019); the NNFFT goes back to Elbel and Steidl (1998).\nThe continuous Kaiser–Bessel window is that of Potts and Tasche (2021).\nThe NFCT and NFST use the definitions of NFFT3 (Keiner, Kunis, Potts, ACM TOMS 36, 2009).\nResampling and adjoint resampling by subproblems in shared memory on GPUs are the methods of cuFINUFFT (Shih et al., 2021); the fixed-point accumulation with integer atomics follows mlx-nufft (M. Lachaine, 2026). The double precision variant processes the nodes of a tile in turn instead of using atomic operations.","title":"Where the ideas come from"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Abstract-Interface","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"AbstractNFFTs.jl defines what an NFFT plan is, independent of how it is computed. NFFT.jl is one implementation, NonuniformFFTs.jl is another. Other libraries can implement the interface themselves; code written against it runs with each of them. The comparison of NFFT.jl with FINUFFT, ducc and NonuniformFFTs.jl on the Performance page uses its own benchmark harness (benchmark/ in the repository), not this interface.\n\n(Image: NFFT.jl package family)\n\nnote: Note\nIf you just want to compute NFFTs, use NFFT.jl and ignore this page.","title":"Abstract Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Backends","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"Each implementation has a backend type. Loading NFFT.jl activates its backend; others are chosen explicitly, globally or within a scope:\n\nplan_nfft(k, N)                                  # active backend\nplan_nfft(NFFT.backend(), k, N)                  # explicit backend\nAbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!(NFFT.backend())\nNFFT.activate!()                                 # the same, by convention\nwith(nfft_backend => OtherPackage.backend()) do  # within a scope\n  nfft(k, f)\nend","title":"Backends"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Options:-generic-and-specific","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"Plan constructors take two kinds of keyword arguments:\n\nGeneric options mean the same for every implementation and must be accepted by all of them:\nkeyword meaning\nreltol requested relative error (ell^2 over random data), default 1e-8\ndims dimensions to transform: :, an integer or a contiguous range; the others are batch dimensions\neltype real element type of the data, default from the nodes\nstorage array type of data and workspace (Array, a GPU array type), default from the nodes\nnthreads number of CPU threads, default Threads.nthreads()\nSpecific options belong to one implementation, such as W, σ and window of NFFT.jl. An implementation must reject options it does not know with an ArgumentError, never ignore them silently. Code that uses only generic options is portable.","title":"Options: generic and specific"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Operations","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"function meaning\nmul!(fHat, p, f), p * f NFFT\nmul!(f, adjoint(p), fHat), adjoint(p) * fHat adjoint NFFT (transpose for NFCT/NFST)\nsize_in(p), size_out(p), size(p) array sizes; size(p) is the matrix size\nnodes(p), nodes!(p, k) query and exchange the nodes\naccuracy(p) predicted relative error\nnfft(k, f), nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat), … one-shot functions that create a plan\n\nThe stage interface is optional and meant for implementations that are built from the three classical stages.","title":"Operations"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Writing-an-implementation","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"An implementation defines a backend, a plan type and the operations:\n\nstruct MyBackend <: AbstractNFFTBackend end\nactivate!() = AbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!(MyBackend())\nbackend() = MyBackend()\n\nmutable struct MyPlan{T,D,R} <: AbstractNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n  ...\nend\n\nfunction AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfft(b::MyBackend, k::AbstractMatrix, N::Dims; kwargs...)\n  g, rest = generic_options(k, N; kwargs...)     # reltol, dims, eltype, storage, nthreads\n  check_options(b, rest, (:myoption,))           # reject everything else\n  ...\nend\n\nAbstractNFFTs.size_in(p::MyPlan) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.size_out(p::MyPlan) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.nodes(p::MyPlan) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.nodes!(p::MyPlan, k) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.accuracy(p::MyPlan) = ...\nLinearAlgebra.mul!(fHat, p::MyPlan, f) = ...\nLinearAlgebra.mul!(f, p::Adjoint{<:Complex,<:MyPlan}, fHat) = ...\n\nIn the type parameters, T is the real element type of the data, D the number of dimensions of the input and R that of the output (R = 1 without batch dimensions). Everything else, *, nfft, size and the rules for automatic differentiation (ChainRules for Zygote, Enzyme), is derived from these functions.\n\nThe test suite of NFFT.jl contains contract tests (test/contract.jl) that check an implementation against the NDFT, for several tolerances reltol and with batch dimensions (dims); they take a plan constructor as an argument and can be run against any backend.","title":"Writing an implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#GPU","page":"GPU","text":"This guide shows how to run the transforms of NFFT.jl on a GPU. All transforms (NFFT, RNFFT, NFCT, NFST, NNFFT) run there, including batch dimensions. The kernels are written with KernelAbstractions.jl and the FFTs go through AbstractFFTs, so the same code serves CUDA.jl, AMDGPU.jl and Metal.jl arrays. The GPU code is a package extension that is loaded together with the GPU package.","title":"GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Create-a-plan-on-the-GPU","page":"GPU","text":"A plan works on the GPU when its storage is a GPU array type: give the nodes as a GPU array, or pass storage with nodes on the CPU.\n\nusing NFFT, CUDA                     # or AMDGPU, Metal\n\nk = rand(Float32, 2, 100_000) .- 0.5f0\nf = CUDA.randn(ComplexF32, 256, 256)\n\np = plan_nfft(k, size(f); reltol=1e-5, storage=CuArray)   # or plan_nfft(CuArray(k), size(f))\nfHat = p * f                         # CuArray\ng = adjoint(p) * fHat\nnodes(p)                             # the nodes as a CuArray (in the precision of the plan)\n\nThe other transforms take the same option:\n\nplan_rnfft(k, (256, 256); storage=CuArray)\nplan_nfct(abs.(k), (256, 256); storage=CuArray)\nplan_nnfft(k, y; storage=CuArray)\nplan_nfft(k, (256, 256, 16); dims=1:2, storage=CuArray)   # 16 coils in one call\n\nsdc from NFFTTools.jl and the stage interface work with GPU plans as well. Data on the CPU passed to a GPU plan raise an error instead of being copied.\n\nThe options fft and tilesize belong to the CPU code; GPU plans reject them. Timings are on the Performance page, the kernels are explained in How NFFT.jl Works.","title":"Create a plan on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Parameter-choice-on-a-GPU","page":"GPU","text":"A plan chooses window width and oversampling factor by a cost model (see Accuracy and Parameters). GPUs have their own: resampling, FFT and fixed costs weigh differently there than on the CPU. It is fitted on an Apple M1 Max. For another GPU, NFFT.calibrate_cost times NFFTs for a range of parameters (about three minutes) and makes the plans of the session use the fit:\n\ncoef = NFFT.calibrate_cost(CuArray; eltype=Float32)\nNFFT.set_cost_model!(CuArray, Float32, coef)   # in a later session\n\nmeasure = true times the four best candidates of the cost model when the plan is created.","title":"Parameter choice on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Run-the-GPU-tests","page":"GPU","text":"test/gpu.jl compares every transform on the GPU with the CPU plan. It runs with\n\njulia test/gpu/run.jl cuda      # or rocm, metal\n\nin a temporary environment with the GPU package.","title":"Run the GPU tests"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Transforms-along-Dimensions","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"This guide shows how to transform some dimensions of an array and keep the others, for example the coils of a multi-coil MRI acquisition, the A-scans of an OCT B-scan, time frames or channels. With dims a plan transforms along a contiguous range of dimensions; all other dimensions are batch dimensions and pass through unchanged. How the plan processes the batches is explained in How NFFT.jl Works.","title":"Transforms along Dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Transform-one-dimension-of-a-matrix","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"A 1D NFFT along the first dimension of a 16  20 matrix, at 11 nodes:\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\ny = rand(11) .- 0.5\nf = randn(ComplexF64, 16, 20)\np1 = plan_nfft(y, size(f); dims=1)\nsize(p1 * f)\n(11, 20)\n\nThe nodes replace the transformed dimension. Along the second dimension:\n\np2 = plan_nfft(y, size(f); dims=2)\nsize(p2 * f)\n(16, 11)\n\nThe result is one NFFT per column (or row):\n\nq = plan_nfft(y, 16)\np1 * f ≈ reduce(hcat, [q * f[:, c] for c in 1:20])\ntrue","title":"Transform one dimension of a matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Transform-all-coils-of-an-MRI-acquisition","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"Images of L receive coils are stored as an N_1  N_2  L array, and every coil is sampled at the same 2D trajectory. A plan with dims = 1:2 transforms all coils in one call and returns a J  L array:\n\nL = 8\nN = (128, 128)\nk = rand(2, 20_000) .- 0.5\ncoils = randn(ComplexF64, N..., L)\np = plan_nfft(k, size(coils); dims=1:2)\ndata = p * coils\nsize(data)\n(20000, 8)\n\nThe adjoint maps the J  L data back to the coil images:\n\nsize(adjoint(p) * data)\n(128, 128, 8)\n\nOne plan for all coils is faster than a loop over the coils with a plan for one image: depending on the number of threads it computes the window weights of a node once for all coils, or runs the coils in parallel:\n\nsingle = plan_nfft(k, N)\nout = similar(data)\nloop!(out, single, coils) = for c in 1:L\n  mul!(view(out, :, c), single, view(coils, :, :, c))\nend\nloop!(out, single, coils); mul!(data, p, coils)        # compile\nt_loop = minimum(@elapsed(loop!(out, single, coils)) for _ in 1:5)\nt_batch = minimum(@elapsed(mul!(data, p, coils)) for _ in 1:5)\n(loop_ms=1e3t_loop, batch_ms=1e3t_batch, agree=out ≈ data)\n(loop_ms = 7.295792, batch_ms = 2.90457, agree = true)","title":"Transform all coils of an MRI acquisition"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Transform-many-short-signals","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"For many small transforms, such as the 512 A-scans of an OCT B-scan, the plan runs whole slices in parallel, one per thread (with several threads, e.g. julia -t auto). Again a single plan with dims does it:\n\nk1 = rand(1, 2048) .- 0.5\npm = plan_nfft(k1, (2048, 512); dims=1)\nf1 = randn(ComplexF64, 2048, 512); y1 = pm * f1\nsingle1 = plan_nfft(k1, 2048)\nloop1!(y, p, f) = for c in axes(f, 2)\n  mul!(view(y, :, c), p, view(f, :, c))\nend\nloop1!(y1, single1, f1)\n(loop_ms=1e3minimum(@elapsed(loop1!(y1, single1, f1)) for _ in 1:5),\n dims_ms=1e3minimum(@elapsed(mul!(y1, pm, f1)) for _ in 1:5))\n(loop_ms = 86.51809200000001, dims_ms = 11.258289)\n\nFor real-valued interferograms, as in OCT, see Real Data.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Transform many short signals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Options","page":"API (Julia)","text":"Keyword arguments of plan_nfft, plan_rnfft, plan_nfct and plan_nfst; plan_nnfft takes them without tilesize and measure, its dims is the dimension of the sources in an array of the size given as third argument, and its window, W and σ apply to its inner NFFT:\n\nkeyword meaning\nreltol requested relative error, default 1e-8\ndims dimensions to transform, see Transforms along Dimensions\neltype Float32 or Float64, default from the nodes\nstorage array type of data and workspace, default from the nodes; see GPU\nnthreads number of threads, default Threads.nthreads()\nwindow :auto, KaiserBessel(), ExpSemicircle(), CustomWindow(φ)\nW, σ window width and oversampling factor\nfft FFT backend: FFTWBackend() (default) or FFTABackend() (pure Julia, using FFTA; NFFT and NNFFT)\ntilesize edge length of the tiles the nodes are sorted into (expert option)\nmeasure true: time the four parameter sets of lowest predicted cost at plan time and keep the fastest\n\nThe first five are part of the Abstract Interface and mean the same for every implementation; the others are specific to NFFT.jl. An unknown option is an error, and so are fft and tilesize for plans on a GPU. How to use window, W, σ and fft is shown in Choosing Parameters.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Plans-and-transforms","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"Plans and transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Windows-and-FFT","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"Windows and FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Stages","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"Stages"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#NFFTTools","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"NFFTTools"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Direct-transforms","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"Direct transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Abstract-interface","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"Abstract interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API (Julia)","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfft","page":"API (Julia)","text":"plan_nfft([backend,] k, N; reltol=1e-8, dims=:, eltype, storage, nthreads, kwargs...)\n\nPlan an NFFT for the nodes k (a d × J matrix, or a vector for d = 1, entries in [-1/2, 1/2)) and an equidistant array of size N (an integer or a tuple; with dims, the size of the whole array including batch dimensions). Without backend the active backend is used.\n\nGeneric options, accepted by every implementation:\n\nreltol: requested relative error (ℓ² norm over random data)\ndims: dimensions to transform (:, an integer or a contiguous range); the other dimensions are batch dimensions and pass through unchanged\neltype: real element type of the data, default float(eltype(k))\nstorage: array type of data and workspace, default the array type of k\nnthreads: number of CPU threads, default Threads.nthreads()\n\nFurther keyword arguments are specific to the implementation; an implementation throws an ArgumentError for options it does not know.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nnfft","page":"API (Julia)","text":"plan_nnfft([backend,] k, y[, N]; kwargs...)\n\nPlan an NNFFT with non-equidistant nodes k (D × J) in the frequency domain and y (D × M) in the spatial domain. The input is a vector of length M, or an array of size N whose dimension dims (an integer) holds the M sources; the output has the J targets in that dimension, and the other dimensions are batch dimensions. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nnfft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfct","page":"API (Julia)","text":"plan_nfct([backend,] k, N; kwargs...)\n\nPlan a non-equidistant fast cosine transform (NFCT) for nodes k in [0, 1/2] and a real array of size N. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfst","page":"API (Julia)","text":"plan_nfst([backend,] k, N; kwargs...)\n\nPlan a non-equidistant fast sine transform (NFST) for nodes k in [0, 1/2] and a real array of size N. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfst"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_rnfft","page":"API (Julia)","text":"plan_rnfft([backend,] k, N; kwargs...)\n\nPlan an NFFT with real values at the nodes k (in [-1/2, 1/2)) for an array of logical size N (the NFFT counterpart of FFTW's brfft/rfft). The input of the direct transform is the half h of the coefficients with n₁ = 0 … N₁÷2, an array of size (N₁÷2+1, N₂, …) whose other dimensions are indexed as in plan_nfft; the direct transform is\n\nf̂_j = Re Σ_n h_n exp(-2πi n·k_j),\n\nthe adjoint maps real values y at the nodes to h_n = Σ_j y_j exp(2πi n·k_j). The two are adjoint with respect to the real inner product. With dims, the first of the transformed dimensions is the halved one. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_rnfft"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nNFFT plan of NFFT.jl, created by plan_nfft(k, N; kwargs...) or NFFTPlan(k, N; kwargs...).\n\nGeneric options (every AbstractNFFTs implementation): reltol (default 1e-8), dims, eltype, storage, nthreads. Options of NFFT.jl:\n\nwindow: :auto (default; all windows with a parameter table compete), KaiserBessel(), ExpSemicircle() or a CustomWindow (needs W and σ)\nW: window width (integer, odd allowed; W = 2m in the notation of Knopp et al. 2023)\nσ: oversampling factor\nfft: FFT backend, FFTWBackend() (default) or FFTABackend() (using FFTA), see AbstractFFTBackend\ntilesize: expert option, tile edge of the node binning\nmeasure: true times the four parameter sets of lowest predicted cost at plan time (transform and adjoint) and keeps the fastest; the cost model cannot see how fast the FFT is for a particular grid size\n\nT is the real element type, D the number of dimensions of the array and R that of the output (1 without batch dimensions). The compiler infers the type of the plan when dims is left out or a single dimension; with a range it depends on its length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFCTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFCTPlan{T,D,R}\nNFSTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nNFCT and NFST plans of NFFT.jl, created by plan_nfct(k, N; kwargs...) and plan_nfst(k, N; kwargs...), with the definitions of NFFT3: for nodes k in [0, 1/2]^d and a real array f of size N,\n\nNFCT: f̂_j = Σ_{n=0}^{N-1} f_n Π_d cos(2π n_d k_dj),   NFST: f̂_j = Σ_{n=1}^{N} f_n Π_d sin(2π n_d k_dj)\n\n(the array index of mode n is n + 1 for the NFCT and n for the NFST). transpose(p) is the transposed transform. Options as for NFFTPlan; batch dimensions run as slices, in parallel or one after the other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFCTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFSTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFCTPlan{T,D,R}\nNFSTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nNFCT and NFST plans of NFFT.jl, created by plan_nfct(k, N; kwargs...) and plan_nfst(k, N; kwargs...), with the definitions of NFFT3: for nodes k in [0, 1/2]^d and a real array f of size N,\n\nNFCT: f̂_j = Σ_{n=0}^{N-1} f_n Π_d cos(2π n_d k_dj),   NFST: f̂_j = Σ_{n=1}^{N} f_n Π_d sin(2π n_d k_dj)\n\n(the array index of mode n is n + 1 for the NFCT and n for the NFST). transpose(p) is the transposed transform. Options as for NFFTPlan; batch dimensions run as slices, in parallel or one after the other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFSTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.RNFFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"RNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nRNFFT plan of NFFT.jl (real values at the nodes), created by plan_rnfft(k, N; kwargs...), see plan_rnfft. Options as for NFFTPlan; batch dimensions loop over the slices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.RNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NNFFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NNFFTPlan{T,D,M}\n\nNNFFT plan of NFFT.jl (non-equidistant nodes in both domains), created by plan_nnfft(k, y[, N]; kwargs...) for targets k (D × J) and sources y (D × N), arbitrary real coordinates:\n\nf̂_j = Σ_l f_l exp(−2πi k_j·y_l)\n\nThe cost grows with the product of the extents of k and y (the size of the inner NFFT is about 4σ²·Y·K per dimension), not with their numbers. With an array size N (M dimensions), dims selects the dimension of the sources; the transform maps it to the targets and passes the other dimensions through, one slice after the other. Generic options reltol, eltype, storage, nthreads; restrictions of the inner NFFT window (:auto, KaiserBessel() or ExpSemicircle()), W and σ, and its FFT backend fft, as for NFFTPlan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFFTBackend","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFFTBackend()\n\nThe AbstractNFFTs backend of NFFT.jl. Activated when the package is loaded; reactivate with NFFT.activate!().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFFTBackend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFT.parameters","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFFT.parameters(p) -> (; window, W, σ)\n\nWindow family, window width and oversampling factor the plan uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.accuracy","page":"API (Julia)","text":"accuracy(p) -> Float64\n\nRelative error (ℓ² norm over random data) the plan p is expected to achieve; what the requested reltol turned into.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.accuracy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFT.calibrate_cost","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFFT.calibrate_cost(storage=Array; eltype=Float64, J=(2^16, 2^18, 2^20), W=4:2:12,\n                    σ=(1.25, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5), dims=1:3, sizes=CALIBRATION_SIZES, nrep=10, set=true)\n\nFit the cost model by which plans choose window width and oversampling to NFFTs timed on this machine: transform and adjoint for random nodes and every combination of J, W, σ and the sizes in sizes[D] (default 2^16 and 2^20, 256² and 1024², 32³ and 96³) in the dimensions dims. With set, plans with this storage and eltype use the result for the rest of the session. Returns a Dict from the dimension to the coefficients, for NFFT.set_cost_model! in a later session.\n\nThe built-in models are fitted on an Apple M1 Max (CPU with 8 threads, GPU with Metal and Float32). Other machines, thread counts and GPUs have other ratios of resampling, FFT and fixed costs. The default sweep takes about three minutes on the M1 Max GPU; on the CPU, smaller J, sizes and fewer W keep it short.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.calibrate_cost"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFT.set_cost_model!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NFFT.set_cost_model!(storage, eltype, coef)\nNFFT.set_cost_model!(storage, eltype, nothing)\n\nUse the coefficients coef (a Dict from the dimension 1, 2, 3 to a tuple, as returned by NFFT.calibrate_cost) when plans with this storage and eltype choose their parameters; nothing restores the built-in model. The setting holds for the Julia session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.set_cost_model!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nodes","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nodes(p) -> k\n\nNodes of the plan p (a d × J matrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nodes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nodes!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nodes!(p, k) -> p\n\nChange nodes k in the plan p operation and return the plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nodes!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.size_in","page":"API (Julia)","text":"size_in(p)\n\nSize of the input array for the plan p (NFFT/NFCT/NFST/NNFFT).  The returned tuple has D entries. Note that this will be the output size for the transposed / adjoint operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.size_in"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.size_out","page":"API (Julia)","text":"size_out(p)\n\nSize of the output array for the plan p (NFFT/NFCT/NFST/NNFFT).  The returned tuple has R entries.  Note that this will be the input size for the transposed / adjoint operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.size_out"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LinearAlgebra.mul!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"mul!(fHat, p, f) -> fHat\n\nIn-place transform of f (size size_in(p)) into fHat (size size_out(p)).\n\n\n\n\n\nmul!(f, p, fHat) -> f\n\nIn-place adjoint transform of fHat (size size_out(p)) into f (size size_in(p)).\n\n\n\n\n\nmul!(f, p, fHat) -> f\n\nIn-place transposed NFCT/NFST of fHat (size size_out(p)) into f (size size_in(p)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Base.:*","page":"API (Julia)","text":"*(p, f) -> fHat\n*(adjoint(p), fHat) -> f\n*(transpose(p), fHat) -> f\n\nApply a plan, its adjoint (NFFT, NNFFT, RNFFT) or its transpose (NFCT, NFST) and return a new array. f has size size_in(p) and fHat size size_out(p); batch dimensions (dims) are part of both. mul! writes into a given array instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfft","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nfft(k, f; kwargs...)\nnfft(backend, k, f; kwargs...)\n\nNFFT of the array f at the nodes k (D × J, a vector for D = 1), with a plan made for this call: plan_nfft(backend, k, size(f); kwargs...) * f. For repeated transforms at the same nodes, make the plan once.\n\nWithout backend the active backend is used. A package activates itself with BackendModule.activate!(), and a scoped value overrides the active backend within a scope:\n\njulia> NFFT.activate!()\n\njulia> nfft(k, f)                                   # uses NFFT\n\njulia> with(nfft_backend => NonuniformFFTs.backend()) do\n          nfft(k, f)                                # uses NonuniformFFTs\n       end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfft_adjoint","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat; kwargs...)\nnfft_adjoint(backend, k, N, fHat; kwargs...)\n\nAdjoint NFFT of the values fHat at the nodes k, an array of size N: adjoint(plan_nfft(backend, k, N; kwargs...)) * fHat. Backend as for nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfft_adjoint"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfct","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nfct(k, f; kwargs...)\nnfct(backend, k, f; kwargs...)\n\nNFCT of the real array f at the nodes k in 0 12^D: plan_nfct(backend, k, size(f); kwargs...) * f. Backend as for nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfct_transpose","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nfct_transpose(k, N, fHat; kwargs...)\nnfct_transpose(backend, k, N, fHat; kwargs...)\n\nTransposed NFCT of the values fHat at the nodes k, an array of size N: transpose(plan_nfct(backend, k, N; kwargs...)) * fHat. Backend as for nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfct_transpose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfst","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nfst(k, f; kwargs...)\nnfst(backend, k, f; kwargs...)\n\nNFST of the real array f at the nodes k in 0 12^D: plan_nfst(backend, k, size(f); kwargs...) * f. Backend as for nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfst"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfst_transpose","page":"API (Julia)","text":"nfst_transpose(k, N, fHat; kwargs...)\nnfst_transpose(backend, k, N, fHat; kwargs...)\n\nTransposed NFST of the values fHat at the nodes k, an array of size N: transpose(plan_nfst(backend, k, N; kwargs...)) * fHat. Backend as for nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfst_transpose"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.AbstractWindow","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractWindow\n\nWindow function of the NFFT. A window is evaluated in grid units, φ(z) for z ∈ [-W/2, W/2] (W the window width, W = 2m in the notation of Knopp et al. 2023); it is symmetric and zero outside. The resampling evaluates a piecewise polynomial fitted to φ, so the cost of φ itself does not matter.\n\nFamilies with a parameter table (used by the automatic parameter choice): KaiserBessel, ExpSemicircle. Any other window can be used with explicit W and σ through CustomWindow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.AbstractWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.KaiserBessel","page":"API (Julia)","text":"KaiserBessel()\n\nContinuous Kaiser–Bessel window (Potts & Tasche 2021): the sinh form sinh(β√(1−(2z/W)²))/√(1−(2z/W)²) minus its edge value β, so that it vanishes at |z| = W/2. The shape parameter β is taken from the kernel table (optimised per W and σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.KaiserBessel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.ExpSemicircle","page":"API (Julia)","text":"ExpSemicircle()\n\nExponential of semicircle family, φ(z) = exp(β((1−(2z/W)²)^e₀ − 1)). e₀ = 1/2 is the ES kernel of FINUFFT (Barnett et al. 2019); a free exponent e₀ is the family used by ducc (Arras et al. 2021). β and e₀ are taken from the kernel table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.ExpSemicircle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.CustomWindow","page":"API (Julia)","text":"CustomWindow(φ)\n\nUser-defined window: φ(z, W) must be symmetric in z and zero for |z| ≥ W/2. Requires explicit W and σ in the plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.CustomWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.AbstractFFTBackend","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractFFTBackend\n\nFFT used for the oversampled grid by NFFT plans (and the NNFFT): batched 1D transforms along one dimension of an array view, in place as seen from NFFT.jl. A backend B implements\n\nNFFT.pass_plan(::B, ::Val{K}, v, d): a plan for the transform of the view v along dimension d, forward for K = -1, unnormalised backward for K = 1\nNFFT.apply_pass!(plan, v): apply it to v in place (default: plan * v, an in-place plan)\noptionally NFFT.planning(f, ::B, nthreads): run the planning f() with nthreads threads per plan (default: f())\n\nThe RNFFT, NFCT and NFST use FFTW's real and real-to-real transforms and need FFTWBackend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.AbstractFFTBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.FFTWBackend","page":"API (Julia)","text":"FFTWBackend(; flags = FFTW.ESTIMATE)\n\nFFTW on the CPU, the default. In D ≥ 2 NFFT.jl threads the per-dimension passes itself over single-threaded FFTW plans: FFTW's own threading with ESTIMATE produces plans up to 17× slower than MEASURE, while single-threaded batched ESTIMATE plans are within 10–20 % of MEASURE. A transform with one dimension uses FFTW's own threading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.FFTWBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.FFTABackend","page":"API (Julia)","text":"FFTABackend()\n\nFFTA.jl, an FFT in pure Julia; needs using FFTA. For NFFT and NNFFT plans. Slower than FFTW; its plans work out of place, so every pass keeps a buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.FFTABackend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_size","page":"API (Julia)","text":"oversampled_size(p) -> NTuple\n\nSize of the oversampled equidistant array the FFT of plan p acts on. The stage interface is defined for plans without batch dimensions; an implementation throws an ArgumentError for others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.spectrum_size","page":"API (Julia)","text":"spectrum_size(p) -> NTuple\n\nSize of the oversampled spectrum ĝ that the resampling correction writes and the FFT stage reads: oversampled_size(p), except for RNFFT plans (the half spectrum (Ñ₁÷2+1, Ñ₂, …)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.spectrum_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"resampling_correction!(p, f, ĝ) -> ĝ\n\nFirst step of the direct NFFT: divide f by the Fourier transform of the window function and place it into the zero-padded oversampled spectrum ĝ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_fft!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"oversampled_fft!(p, g) -> g\noversampled_fft!(p, ĝ, g) -> g\n\nSecond step of the direct NFFT: the FFT of the oversampled spectrum, in place on g or from ĝ into g. The out-of-place form leaves ĝ unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_fft!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"resampling!(p, g, fHat) -> fHat\n\nThird step of the direct NFFT: resample the oversampled equidistant array g at the nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling_adjoint!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"resampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g) -> g\n\nFirst step of the adjoint NFFT: the adjoint resampling of the values fHat at the nodes onto the oversampled equidistant array g (overwritten).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling_adjoint!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_bfft!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"oversampled_bfft!(p, g) -> g\noversampled_bfft!(p, g, ĝ) -> ĝ\n\nSecond step of the adjoint NFFT: the adjoint (unnormalised backward) FFT, in place on g or from g into ĝ. The out-of-place form leaves g unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_bfft!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction_adjoint!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"resampling_correction_adjoint!(p, ĝ, f) -> f\n\nThird step of the adjoint NFFT: crop the oversampled spectrum ĝ and divide by the Fourier transform of the window function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction_adjoint!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SparseArrays.sparse-Tuple{NFFTPlan}","page":"API (Julia)","text":"sparse(p::NFFTPlan) -> B\n\nThe resampling stage of the plan p (without batch dimensions, on the CPU) as a sparse J × prod(oversampled_size(p)) matrix of window weights, W^D entries per node: for an oversampled array g, resampling!(p, g, fHat) computes fHat = B * vec(g) and resampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g) computes vec(g) = B' * fHat. Requires using SparseArrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseArrays.sparse"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.sdc","page":"API (Julia)","text":"weights = sdc(p::AbstractNFFTPlan; method = :pipemenon, iters)\n\nSampling density compensation weights for the nodes of the NFFT plan p, scaled such that A^mathsfH operatornamediag(w) A bm1  bm1. For nodes on the equidistant grid of an array of size N they are ≈ 1/prod(N).\n\nmethod = :pipemenon (default): iters (default 20) iterations of the method of Pipe and Menon (Magn Reson Med 41(1):179–186, 1999, doi:10.1002/(SICI)1522-2594(199901)41:1<179::AID-MRM25>3.0.CO;2-V). The iteration uses the resampling stage of the plan, i.e. the window function on the oversampled grid, as its kernel. The weights therefore depend on the window of the plan rather than on its accuracy: narrow windows give better weights than wide ones. With NFFT.jl, plan_nfft(k, N; W = 4, σ = 2.0) works well (MRI test data: 0.11 relative deviation of a gridding reconstruction from the scanner's, 0.21 with the window chosen for reltol = 1e-3). The weights are positive. p must implement the stage interface of AbstractNFFTs (oversampled_size, resampling!, resampling_adjoint!).\n\nmethod = :frobenius: the weights that minimise A^mathsfH operatornamediag(w) A - I_F (Sedarat and Nishimura, IEEE Trans Med Imaging 19(4):306–317, 2000), the best a diagonal weighting can do. With at least as many nodes as the doubled array has modes (prod(2 .* N)), spread without clusters, they reconstruct every array exactly. They solve S w = I_N bm1 with S_jh = (A A^mathsfH)_jh^2: the entries of A A^mathsfH are the Dirichlet kernel at x_j - x_h, their squares the Fejér kernel, a trigonometric polynomial on the doubled array with coefficients c_l = prod_d (N_d - l_d). Hence S = A_2N operatornamediag(c) A_2N^mathsfH costs one NFFT and one adjoint NFFT of size 2 .* N, and iters (default 50) steps of conjugate gradients minimise the Frobenius error. The weights can be negative. The plan of size 2 .* N is made with the active backend.\n\np must be a plan without batch dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.sdc"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.sdc!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"sdc!(weights, p::AbstractNFFTPlan; iters = 20, workg, workf, workv)\n\nIn-place version of sdc. The work arrays (complex, of sizes oversampled_size(p), size_out(p) and size_in(p)) can be passed to avoid allocations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.sdc!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel","page":"API (Julia)","text":"calculateToeplitzKernel(shape, tr::AbstractMatrix{T}; fftplan = plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE), kwargs...)\n\nCalculate the kernel for an implementation of the Gram matrix A^mathsfH A that utilizes its Toeplitz structure. The output is an array of twice the size of shape, as the Toeplitz trick requires an oversampling factor of 2 (cf. Wajer, F. T. A. W., and K. P. Pruessmann. \"Major speedup of reconstruction for sensitivity encoding with arbitrary trajectories.\" Proc. Intl. Soc. Mag. Res. Med. 2001.). The type of the kernel is Complex{T}, i.e. the complex of the k-space trajectory's type; for speed and memory efficiency, call this function with Float32.(tr), and the kernel will also be Float32.\n\nThe kernel is computed with an adjoint NFFT of the active AbstractNFFTs backend (e.g. NFFT.jl after using NFFT).\n\nRequired Arguments\n\nshape::NTuple(Int): size of the image; e.g. (256, 256) for 2D imaging, or (256,256,128) for 3D imaging\ntr::Matrix{T}: non-Cartesian k-space trajectory in units revolutions/voxel, i.e. tr[i] ∈ [-0.5, 0.5] ∀ i. The matrix has the size 2 x Nsamples for 2D imaging with a trajectory length Nsamples, and 3 x Nsamples for 3D imaging.\n\nKeyword Arguments:\n\nfftplan: plan for the final FFT of the kernel from image to k-space. Therefore, it has to have twice the size of the original image. default = plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE). If this constructor is used many times, it is worth to precompute the plan with plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.MEASURE) and reuse it.\nkwargs: passed on to plan_nfft, e.g. reltol for the accuracy of the kernel.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using NFFT, NFFTTools\n\njulia> Nx = 32;\n\njulia> trj = Float32.(rand(2, 1000) .- 0.5);\n\njulia> λ = calculateToeplitzKernel((Nx, Nx), trj);\n\njulia> y = randn(ComplexF32, Nx, Nx);\n\njulia> convolveToeplitzKernel!(y, λ);\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"calculateToeplitzKernel!(f::AbstractArray{Complex{T}}, p::AbstractNFFTPlan, tr::AbstractMatrix{T}, fftplan)\n\nCalculate the kernel for an implementation of the Gram matrix that utilizes its Toeplitz structure and writes it in-place in f, which has to be twice the size of the desired image matrix, as the Toeplitz trick requires an oversampling factor of 2 (cf. Wajer, F. T. A. W., and K. P. Pruessmann. \"Major speedup of reconstruction for sensitivity encoding with arbitrary trajectories.\" Proc. Intl. Soc. Mag. Res. Med. 2001.). The type of the kernel f has to be Complex{T}, i.e. the complex of the k-space trajectory's type; for speed and memory efficiecy, call this function with Float32.(tr), and set the type of f accordingly.\n\nRequired Arguments\n\nf::AbstractArray{Complex{T}}: array in which the kernel will be written.\np::AbstractNFFTPlan: NFFT plan for an array of twice the image size; its nodes are set to tr.\ntr::Matrix{T}: non-Cartesian k-space trajectory in units revolutions/voxel, i.e. tr[i] ∈ [-0.5, 0.5] ∀ i. The matrix has the size 2 x Nsamples for 2D imaging with a trajectory length Nsamples, and 3 x Nsamples for 3D imaging.\nfftplan: plan for the final FFT of the kernel from image to k-space. Therefore, it has to have twice the size of the original image. Calculate, e.g., with fftplan = plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.MEASURE), where shape is the size of the reconstructed image.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using NFFT, NFFTTools, NFFTTools.FFTW\n\njulia> Nx = 32;\n\njulia> trj = Float32.(rand(2, 1000) .- 0.5);\n\njulia> p = plan_nfft(trj, (2Nx, 2Nx));\n\njulia> fftplan = plan_fft(zeros(ComplexF32, (2Nx,2Nx)); flags=FFTW.MEASURE);\n\njulia> λ = Array{ComplexF32}(undef, 2Nx, 2Nx);\n\njulia> calculateToeplitzKernel!(λ, p, trj, fftplan);\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.convolveToeplitzKernel!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"convolveToeplitzKernel!(y::AbstractArray{T,N}, λ::AbstractArray{T,N}[, fftplan = plan_fft(λ; flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE), ifftplan = plan_ifft(λ; flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE), xOS1 = similar(λ), xOS2 = similar(λ)])\n\nConvolves the image y with the Toeplitz kernel λ and overwrites y with the result. y is also returned for convenience. As this function is commonly applied many times, it is highly recommended to pre-allocate / pre-compute all optional arguments. By doing so, this entire function is non-allocating.\n\nRequired Arguments\n\ny::Array{T,N}: Input image that will be overwritten with the result. y is a matrix (N=2) for 2D imaging and a 3D tensor (N=3) for 3D imaging. The type of the elements T must match the ones of λ.\nλ::Array{T,N}: Toeplitz kernel, which has the same element type as y and twice its size due to the required oversampling (cf. calculateToeplitzKernel).\n\nOptional, but highly recommended Arguments\n\nfftplan: plan for the oversampled FFT, i.e. it has to have twice the size of the original image. Calculate, e.g., with fftplan = plan_fft(λ; flags=FFTW.MEASURE).\nifftplan: plan for the oversampled inverse FFT. Calculate, e.g., with ifftplan = plan_ifft(λ; flags=FFTW.MEASURE).\nxOS1: pre-allocated array of the size of λ. Pre-allocate with xOS1 = similar(λ).\nxOS2: pre-allocated array of the size of λ. Pre-allocate with xOS2 = similar(λ).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using NFFT, NFFTTools, NFFTTools.FFTW\n\njulia> Nx = 32;\n\njulia> trj = Float32.(rand(2, 1000) .- 0.5);\n\njulia> λ = calculateToeplitzKernel((Nx, Nx), trj);\n\njulia> xOS1 = similar(λ);\n\njulia> xOS2 = similar(λ);\n\njulia> fftplan = plan_fft(xOS1; flags=FFTW.MEASURE);\n\njulia> ifftplan = plan_ifft(xOS1; flags=FFTW.MEASURE);\n\njulia> y = randn(ComplexF32, Nx, Nx);\n\njulia> convolveToeplitzKernel!(y, λ, fftplan, ifftplan, xOS1, xOS2);\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.convolveToeplitzKernel!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NDFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems. Nodes are a D × J matrix or, for D = 1, a vector; the sums are computed in double precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NDFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NDCTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems. Nodes are a D × J matrix or, for D = 1, a vector; the sums are computed in double precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NDCTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NDSTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems. Nodes are a D × J matrix or, for D = 1, a vector; the sums are computed in double precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NDSTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NNDFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems. Nodes are a D × J matrix or, for D = 1, a vector; the sums are computed in double precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NNDFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for any NFFT-like plan (NFFT, NNFFT, RNFFT, NFCT, NFST).\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractComplexFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractComplexFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for either an NFFT plan or an NNFFT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractComplexFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRealFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractRealFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for either an NFCT plan or an NFST plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRealFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFFT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNNFFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractNNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NNFFT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFCTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractNFCTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFCT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFCTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFSTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractNFSTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFST plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFSTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRNFFTPlan","page":"API (Julia)","text":"AbstractRNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFFT plan with real values at the nodes (the NFFT counterpart of FFTW's brfft/rfft): the direct transform maps the half n₁ ≥ 0 of the complex coefficients to real values at the nodes, the adjoint maps real values at the nodes to that half.\n\nT is the real element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRNFFTPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!","page":"API (Julia)","text":"set_active_backend!(back::Union{Missing, Module, AbstractNFFTBackend})\n\nSet the default NFFT plan backend. A module back must implement back.backend().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.DEFAULT_RELTOL","page":"API (Julia)","text":"DEFAULT_RELTOL\n\nRelative error requested from a plan when reltol is not given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.DEFAULT_RELTOL"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.GenericOptions","page":"API (Julia)","text":"GenericOptions\n\nThe options every implementation of the interface accepts, resolved from the keyword arguments of plan_nfft and friends by generic_options:\n\nreltol: requested relative error (ℓ² norm over random data)\ndims: dimensions of the transform (a contiguous range); all other dimensions are batch dimensions that pass through unchanged\neltype: real element type of the data (Float32, Float64, …)\nstorage: array type of the data and the workspace (Array, a GPU array type, …)\nnthreads: number of CPU threads\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.GenericOptions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.generic_options","page":"API (Julia)","text":"generic_options(k, N; reltol, dims, eltype, storage, nthreads) -> (GenericOptions, rest)\n\nResolve the generic keyword arguments of a plan constructor for nodes k and array size N, and return the remaining (implementation-specific) keyword arguments untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.generic_options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.check_options","page":"API (Julia)","text":"check_options(backend, kwargs, supported)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if kwargs contains a keyword that is not in supported. Every implementation must reject options it does not understand instead of ignoring them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.check_options"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"NFFT.jl is compared with FINUFFT, ducc, NonuniformFFTs.jl and its previous version 0.14. All numbers on this page come from the benchmark suite in benchmark/ of the repository and can be reproduced with it (see Reproducing).\n\nSetup of the figures: Apple M1 Max, 8 threads (performance cores), Julia 1.13; FINUFFT and ducc built from their main branches with native CPU flags; FFTW plans with ESTIMATE. Unless stated otherwise J = bmN = 2^18 uniformly random nodes, bmN = 2^18, 512^2, 64^3, double precision. NonuniformFFTs.jl has no tolerance parameter; its kernel width is derived from the tolerance as in NFFT.jl 0.14.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Time-against-accuracy","page":"Performance","text":"The libraries interpret their tolerance parameters differently, so they are compared at equal measured error. Each point is one tolerance of a sweep from 10^-2 to 10^-14 (single precision: 10^-6); further right means more accurate, lower means faster.\n\n(Image: Time against accuracy, double precision)\n\n1D: NFFT.jl is the fastest library at every accuracy, 1.4–2× ahead of FINUFFT and ducc.\n2D: NFFT.jl and ducc are within 10 % of each other (NFFT.jl ahead at low accuracy), FINUFFT is 1.2–1.6× slower.\n3D: NFFT.jl and ducc are on par; FINUFFT is up to 4.5× slower for the direct transform.\n\n(Image: Time against accuracy, single precision)\n\nIn single precision NFFT.jl is 1.2–1.8× faster than ducc in every dimension. FINUFFT cannot reach small errors in single precision for these sizes (its tolerance floor grows with N; in 1D it rejects even 10^-2).","title":"Time against accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Threads","page":"Performance","text":"Speedup over one thread at a tolerance of 10^-8 (solid: direct, dashed: adjoint; dotted: ideal).\n\n(Image: Strong scaling)\n\nWith 8 threads NFFT.jl reaches a speedup of 4.4–4.7 in 1D, 5.7–6.2 in 2D and 6.3 in 3D; ducc and FINUFFT scale similarly in 2D and 3D and less in 1D (3.2–3.6).","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Problem-size","page":"Performance","text":"Time per node for J = bmN uniformly random nodes at a tolerance of 10^-8.\n\n(Image: Problem size)\n\nFor large problems the time per node is 7–10 ns in 1D and 2D and about 30 ns (direct) and 55 ns (adjoint) in 3D. Below about 10^4 nodes the fixed costs of the threaded stages dominate; there NFFT.jl is slower than ducc in 2D and 3D.","title":"Problem size"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Node-distributions","page":"Performance","text":"Uniformly random nodes, radial spokes through the centre (as in MRI) and nodes clustered in the centre (normal distribution), tolerance 10^-8. Solid bars: direct transform, light bars: adjoint.\n\n(Image: Node distributions)\n\nThe distribution of the nodes hardly matters for any of the libraries.","title":"Node distributions"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Many-transforms-with-the-same-nodes","page":"Performance","text":"Time per transform for L transforms with the same nodes (dims, FINUFFT's ntrans; ducc loops over the transforms), tolerance 10^-8.\n\n(Image: Batches)\n\nTransforms with the same nodes share the window evaluation. For many short 1D transforms (OCT: 512 A-scans) NFFT.jl runs the transforms in parallel on single-threaded copies of the plan and needs 7 µs per transform, 13–15× less than FINUFFT and ducc. For multi-coil MRI it is 1.1–2× faster than the others, for volumes on par with ducc.","title":"Many transforms with the same nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Real-data","page":"Performance","text":"The RNFFT (real values at the nodes), the NFCT and the NFST against the complex NFFT of the same size, tolerance 10^-8.\n\n(Image: Real data)\n\nThe RNFFT computes on a real grid and needs about 60 % of the time of the complex NFFT. NFCT and NFST cost about as much as the complex NFFT in 2D and 3D; in 1D they are still slower.","title":"Real data"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Non-equidistant-in-both-domains-(NNFFT)","page":"Performance","text":"The NNFFT (FINUFFT's type 3) maps values at arbitrary sources bmy_l to arbitrary targets bmk_j. Here bmN sources uniformly random in prod_d -N_d2 N_d2) and as many targets in -12 12)^D, the space-frequency extent of an NFFT of size bmN (2^16, 256^2, 32^3); tolerance sweep as above.\n\n(Image: NNFFT, double precision)\n\nIn double precision NFFT.jl is faster than FINUFFT in 1D (up to 1.5×) and in 2D, and on par in 3D, where FINUFFT is ahead around 10^-10 in the adjoint and NFFT.jl at the other accuracies. At the highest accuracy the difference is large: in 2D NFFT.jl reaches 710^-14 in 3.6 ms, FINUFFT 910^-13 in 22 ms. Planning takes 5–12 ms in NFFT.jl and 4–7 ms in FINUFFT (more at its accuracy limits).\n\n(Image: NNFFT, single precision)\n\nNFFT.jl computes grid positions and phases in double precision also for single-precision data. With the large coordinates of the type 3 this matters: NFFT.jl reaches 310^-7 in 1D, 2D and 3D, where FINUFFT stays at 10^-2 (1D), 510^-5 (2D) and 810^-6 (3D). At equal error NFFT.jl is also faster, except in 3D at errors above 10^-4.","title":"Non-equidistant in both domains (NNFFT)"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#On-GPUs","page":"Performance","text":"Apple M1 Max (Metal, single precision, 2^18 nodes, tolerance 10^-4) against the CPU with 8 threads, ms (direct / adjoint; fastest of repeated runs into preallocated outputs, the GPU synchronised after each):\n\n CPU GPU\nNFFT, 1D 2^18 0.9 / 0.8 0.5 / 0.7\nNFFT, 2D 512^2 1.3 / 1.3 0.7 / 0.9\nNFFT, 3D 64^3 3.1 / 3.6 1.0 / 1.6\nRNFFT, 3D 2.9 / 2.6 1.0 / 1.3\nNFFT, OCT 2048 times 512 2.4 / 2.4 1.0 / 1.5\n\nmlx-nufft, with the same subproblem scheme (see How NFFT.jl Works), takes 0.6 / 0.4, 0.6 / 0.7 and 0.9 / 1.7 ms for the first three rows, at errors 2 to 40 times larger than NFFT.jl's for the same tolerance (run metal of the benchmark suite). Measurements on Nvidia and AMD GPUs are still missing.","title":"On GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#NFFT.jl-1.0-against-0.14","page":"Performance","text":"(Image: Speedup over NFFT.jl 0.14)\n\nSpeedup of NFFT.jl 1.0 over 0.14 at the same requested tolerance, with 1 thread (dashed) and 8 threads (solid). NFFT.jl 0.14 misses its tolerance by a factor of 3–4 (e.g. 3510^-4 for 10^-4), so at equal error the speedups are larger. The large factors in 2D with 8 threads come from FFTW's slow threaded ESTIMATE plans that 0.14 used.","title":"NFFT.jl 1.0 against 0.14"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Explorer","page":"Performance","text":"All measurements of this page. Choose a suite, the quantity on the y axis and the parameters; hovering a point shows the details (including the window width W and oversampling factor σ NFFT.jl chose).\n\n<div id=\"nfft-explorer\"></div>","title":"Explorer"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Reproducing","page":"Performance","text":"julia benchmark/bench.jl run fast                  # ≈ 15 s, NFFT.jl alone\njulia benchmark/bench.jl run quick                 # ≈ 1 min, NFFT.jl, FINUFFT and ducc\njulia benchmark/bench.jl run all --save            # all suites of this page, ≈ 15 min\njulia benchmark/bench.jl report                    # figures and explorer data\njulia benchmark/bench.jl ab v1.0.0                 # the working tree against a release\n\nEach library runs in its own process and environment, with its default parameters apart from the tolerance and the number of threads. The error is the relative ell^2 error against the exact NDFT on 64 sampled outputs, the time the minimum over repeated calls of a precomputed plan. Details in benchmark/README.md.","title":"Reproducing"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/rnfft/#Real-Data","page":"Real Data","text":"This guide shows how to transform real values at the nodes with plan_rnfft, the NFFT counterpart of FFTW's rfft/brfft. Real values at the nodes mean Hermitian coefficients, and the plan works with the half n_1 = 0 dots lfloor N_12 rfloor of them (definition in Mathematical Background, implementation in How NFFT.jl Works).\n\nFor an array of logical size bmN the input of the plan has size (lfloor N_12 rfloor + 1 N_2 dots); the other dimensions are indexed as in plan_nfft. The direct transform returns real values, the adjoint takes real values. The two are adjoint with respect to the real inner product, so they can be used in iterative solvers.","title":"Real Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/rnfft/#Reconstruct-OCT-depth-profiles","page":"Real Data","text":"In spectral-domain OCT, each A-scan is a real interferogram sampled at wavenumbers that are not equidistant; the depth profile is its Fourier transform. With dims = 1 one plan reconstructs all A-scans at once. A synthetic B-scan with two reflecting layers:\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nJ, Nz, L = 2048, 1024, 256                      # samples per A-scan, depth, A-scans\nk = reshape(sort(0.45 .* sin.(range(-π / 2, π / 2; length=J))), 1, J)   # non-linear in k\ndepth(l) = (180 + round(Int, 20sin(2π * l / L)), 420 + round(Int, 30cos(2π * l / L)))\ny = zeros(J, L)\nfor l in 1:L, (z, a) in zip(depth(l), (1.0, 0.6))\n  y[:, l] .+= a .* cospi.(2z .* k[1, :])\nend\ny .+= 0.2 .* randn(J, L)\n2048×256 Matrix{Float64}:\n  0.412387   -1.64756  -1.85114  …  -1.34529   0.250488   0.671186\n  0.455682   -1.71167  -1.79482     -1.43751   0.301409   0.367789\n  0.280843   -1.4352   -1.26304     -1.2421    0.456857   0.605467\n  0.409372   -1.37064  -1.42736     -1.33699   0.0826031  0.294426\n  0.617285   -1.13178  -1.81106     -1.4508   -0.0616679  0.496799\n  0.0849957  -1.34479  -1.77687  …  -1.55691   0.653185   0.621613\n  0.435828   -1.64793  -1.65832     -1.49115   0.652055   0.243922\n  0.574261   -1.31171  -1.64193     -1.4526    0.298925   0.496229\n -0.156035   -1.47101  -1.39264     -1.85745   0.35623    0.295452\n  0.0248325  -1.80818  -1.7511      -1.17618   0.113407   0.30618\n  ⋮                              ⋱                        ⋮\n  0.679569   -1.86003  -1.6504      -1.41223   0.522395   0.294318\n  0.486614   -1.54224  -1.46935  …  -1.6947    0.153853   0.469989\n  0.461282   -1.82101  -1.39765     -1.55561   0.457367   0.239671\n  0.493735   -1.34551  -1.67179     -1.54374   0.561056   0.44087\n  0.638923   -1.68852  -1.52832     -1.34948   0.478463   0.393733\n  0.448934   -1.40968  -1.50986     -1.55543   0.864329   0.494264\n  0.533523   -1.26084  -1.50699  …  -1.8455    0.458042   0.68596\n  0.466258   -1.83753  -1.3666      -1.5649    0.576231   0.395772\n  0.504592   -1.63918  -1.32497     -1.32456   0.519996   0.0372952\n\nThe depth profiles are the adjoint applied to the interferograms, weighted with the spacing of the wavenumbers (a simple density compensation):\n\np = plan_rnfft(k, (2Nz, L); dims=1)\nw = [k[2] - k[1]; (k[3:end] .- k[1:end-2]) ./ 2; k[end] - k[end-1]]\nh = adjoint(p) * (w .* y)                       # depth profiles, n = 0 … Nz\nsize(h)\n(1025, 256)\n\ndB = 20log10.(abs.(h) ./ maximum(abs, h))\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 360))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"A-scan\", ylabel=\"depth\", yreversed=true)\nhm = heatmap!(ax, 1:L, 0:Nz, dB'; colormap=:grays, colorrange=(-40, 0))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm; label=\"dB\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWith several threads, the plan runs the 256 A-scans in parallel:\n\nt = minimum(@elapsed(mul!(h, adjoint(p), w .* y)) for _ in 1:5)\n(ms=1e3t, threads=Threads.nthreads())\n(ms = 2.9529780000000003, threads = 4)","title":"Reconstruct OCT depth profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/rnfft/#Check-the-result","page":"Real Data","text":"Against the direct evaluation of the definition:\n\nN = (64, 48)\nkr = rand(2, 500) .- 0.5\npr = plan_rnfft(kr, N)\nhr = randn(ComplexF64, size_in(pr))\nmodes(I) = (I[1] - 1, I[2] - 1 - N[2] ÷ 2)\nref = [sum(real(hr[I] * cispi(-2 * sum(modes(I) .* Tuple(kr[:, j])))) for I in CartesianIndices(hr)) for j in 1:500]\nnorm(pr * hr - ref) / norm(ref), accuracy(pr)\n(3.298142710237964e-9, 5.7478183239441776e-9)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Check the result"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/tools/#Density-Compensation-and-Toeplitz-Kernels","page":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz","text":"This guide shows how to compute sampling density compensation weights and the Toeplitz kernel of the normal operator bmA^mathsfHbmA with NFFTTools.jl. The package builds on the interface of AbstractNFFTs.jl and works with any implementation that provides the stage interface.","title":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz Kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/tools/#Compute-density-compensation-weights","page":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz","text":"The adjoint NFFT is not the inverse of the NFFT. For nodes of non-uniform density, a weighted adjoint bmA^mathsfH operatornamediag(bmw) is a much better approximate inverse. sdc computes weights bmw with the iteration of Pipe and Menon. Here for a radial trajectory, which samples the centre of k-space much more densely than the edge:\n\nusing NFFT, NFFTTools, LinearAlgebra, CairoMakie\n\nN = (64, 64)\nnr, na = 64, 100\nr = range(-0.5, 0.5; length=nr + 1)[1:end-1]\nθ = range(0, π; length=na + 1)[1:end-1]\nk = reduce(hcat, [[ρ * cos(t); ρ * sin(t)] for ρ in r, t in θ]);\n\nThe weights depend on the window of the plan, not on its accuracy; a narrow window gives the best weights:\n\np = plan_nfft(k, N; W=4, σ=2.0)\nw = sdc(p; iters=20)\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 320))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"radius\", ylabel=\"weight\")\nlines!(ax, r, reshape(w, nr, na)[:, 1])\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe weights grow linearly with the radius, as the analytical density compensation of a radial trajectory. A reconstruction of a phantom with the weighted adjoint:\n\nphantom = [hypot(x - 32.5, y - 32.5) < 24 ? 1.0 + (hypot(x - 26, y - 40) < 6) : 0.0 for x in 1:64, y in 1:64]\ndata = p * complex.(phantom)\nrecon = adjoint(p) * (w .* data)\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 320))\nheatmap(fig[1, 1], phantom; colormap=:grays, axis=(aspect=1, title=\"phantom\"))\nheatmap(fig[1, 2], real.(recon); colormap=:grays, axis=(aspect=1, title=\"weighted adjoint\"))\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Compute density compensation weights"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/tools/#Apply-the-normal-operator-with-a-Toeplitz-kernel","page":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz","text":"The normal operator bmA^mathsfHbmA of an NFFT is a Toeplitz matrix, so it can be applied with two FFTs of twice the image size instead of an NFFT and its adjoint. calculateToeplitzKernel computes the kernel once (keyword arguments are passed to plan_nfft), convolveToeplitzKernel! applies it in place:\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nλ = calculateToeplitzKernel(N, k; reltol=1e-10)\ny = convolveToeplitzKernel!(copy(x), λ)\npt = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-10)\nnorm(y - adjoint(pt) * (pt * x)) / norm(y)\n5.133131600006575e-11\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Apply the normal operator with a Toeplitz kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/real/#Cosine-and-Sine-Transforms","page":"Cosine and Sine Transforms","text":"This guide shows how to compute the non-equidistant fast cosine and sine transforms (NFCT, NFST) of real data. NFFT.jl uses the definitions of NFFT3 (see Mathematical Background): nodes lie in 0 12^D, and the array index of mode n_d is n_d + 1 for the NFCT (n_d = 0 dots N_d - 1) and n_d for the NFST (n_d = 1 dots N_d). Instead of an adjoint, the real transforms have a transpose.","title":"Cosine and Sine Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/real/#Transform-and-transpose","page":"Cosine and Sine Transforms","text":"using NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nk = rand(2, 1000) ./ 2            # nodes in [0, 1/2]²\nf = randn(64, 48)\n\np = plan_nfct(k, size(f))\nfHat = p * f\ng = transpose(p) * fHat\nsize(fHat), size(g)\n((1000,), (64, 48))\n\nThe plans take the same options as plan_nfft (reltol, dims, eltype, storage, nthreads, window, W, σ).","title":"Transform and transpose"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/real/#Check-the-result","page":"Cosine and Sine Transforms","text":"Against the direct evaluation:\n\nrelerr(a, b) = norm(a - b) / norm(b)\nrelerr(fHat, NDCTPlan(k, size(f)) * f), accuracy(p)\n(3.5342724978089028e-9, 5.936924253460223e-9)\n\nq = plan_nfst(k, size(f); reltol=1e-12)\nrelerr(q * f, NDSTPlan(k, size(f)) * f), accuracy(q)\n(4.415936037314184e-13, 9.797280318157344e-13)","title":"Check the result"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/real/#Many-transforms-at-once","page":"Cosine and Sine Transforms","text":"With dims, one plan transforms all columns (or slices) at the same nodes:\n\nF = randn(64, 10)\npc = plan_nfct(rand(1, 300) ./ 2, size(F); dims=1)\nsize(pc * F)\n(300, 10)\n\nHow NFFT.jl computes the transforms, as NFFTs of the even or odd extension with a mirrored halo, is explained in How NFFT.jl Works; they cost about as much as a complex NFFT of the same size (see Performance).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Many transforms at once"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Mathematical-Background","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"This page defines the transforms that NFFT.jl computes and the three stages of the algorithm. The derivation and the error analysis are in the NFFT.jl paper, whose notation we follow.\n\n(Image: The transforms of NFFT.jl)\n\nThe NFFT maps an array on an equidistant grid to values at non-equidistant nodes, its adjoint maps back. The other transforms are built on it: the RNFFT restricts it to real values at the nodes, the NFCT and NFST apply it to the even or odd extension of real data, and the NNFFT wraps it into a spreading and a division to reach non-equidistant points on both sides (see Other transforms).\n\nnote: Note\nThe NFFT is also called NUFFT, and in MRI gridding. FINUFFT and others classify the transforms into types: type 2 is the NFFT, type 1 its adjoint, type 3 the NNFFT with non-equidistant points on both sides.","title":"Mathematical Background"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#NDFT","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"Let bmN in mathbbN^D be the size of a D-dimensional array bmf = (f_bmn)_bmn in I_bmN sampled on a grid, indexed by the centred multi-index set\n\nI_bmN = mathbbZ^D cap prod_d=1^D left-fracN_d2fracN_d2right)\n\nThe non-equidistant discrete Fourier transform (NDFT) evaluates the trigonometric polynomial with coefficients f_bmn at J nodes bmk_j in mathbbT^D, where mathbbT = -12 12) is the torus:\n\nhatf_j = sum_bmn in I_bmN f_bmn  mathrme^-2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j\nquad j = 1dotsJ qquad textit(equidistant to non-equidistant)\n\nIts adjoint maps values at the nodes back to the grid,\n\ny_bmn = sum_j=1^J hatf_j  mathrme^2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j\nquad bmn in I_bmN qquad textit(non-equidistant to equidistant)\n\nIn general the adjoint is not the inverse; see Density Compensation and Toeplitz Kernels.\n\nnote: Note\nThe indices in I_bmN are centred around zero, while an ordinary DFT runs over 0dotsN_d-1. An fftshift converts one representation into the other.","title":"NDFT"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Matrix-notation","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"With bmN = prod_d=1^D N_d the NDFT is the matrix–vector product hatbmf = bmA bmf with\n\nbmA = left( mathrme^-2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j right)_j=1dotsJ bmn in I_bmN\nin mathbbC^J times bmN\n\nand the adjoint is bmy = bmA^mathsfH hatbmf. A plan in NFFT.jl behaves like this matrix: p * f, adjoint(p) * fHat, size(p) == (J, prod(N)).","title":"Matrix notation"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#NFFT","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"The direct evaluation costs mathcalO(J bmN) operations. The NFFT approximates bmA by a product of three matrices,\n\nbmA approx bmB bmF bmD\n\nwhich costs mathcalO(tildebmN log tildebmN + J W^D). Here tildebmN approx sigma bmN is the size of an oversampled grid with oversampling factor sigma  1, and W is the width of the window function in grid points. Each matrix is one stage of the algorithm:\n\nResampling correction bmD in mathbbC^tildebmN times bmN: divides bmf by the Fourier transform hatvarphi of the window function and places the result in the zero-padded oversampled array (including the fftshift).\nFFT bmF in mathbbC^tildebmN times tildebmN on the oversampled grid.\nResampling bmB in mathbbC^J times tildebmN: a sparse matrix that interpolates the oversampled grid at each node with the window function varphi, i.e. it combines the W^D grid points around the node.\n\n(Image: The three stages of the NFFT)\n\nThe adjoint NFFT applies the adjoint stages in reverse order,\n\nbmA^mathsfH approx bmD^mathsfH bmF^mathsfH bmB^mathsfH\n\nThe first of them, bmB^mathsfH, adds the weighted value of each node to the grid points around it; this is the costly and hard-to-parallelise part of the adjoint.\n\nnote: Why resampling and not convolution\nThe matrix bmB is often called convolution and bmD deconvolution. Since bmB evaluates the window at node-dependent offsets, it is not shift-invariant and therefore not a convolution. We use the terms resampling and resampling correction, as in the paper.","title":"NFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Window,-width-and-oversampling","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"The accuracy of the approximation depends on three choices: the window function varphi, its width W and the oversampling factor sigma. A larger W or sigma reduces the error, at a higher cost in the resampling (W^D per node) or in the FFT (sigma^D). In the notation of the paper the window has the half width m, so W = 2m; NFFT.jl also allows odd widths.\n\nNFFT.jl chooses all three from the requested relative error, see Accuracy and Parameters.","title":"Window, width and oversampling"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Other-transforms","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"Real values at the nodes (RNFFT). If the values at the nodes are real, the coefficients are Hermitian, f_-bmn = overlinef_bmn, and the half bmh with n_1 = 0 dots lfloor N_12 rfloor determines them. The RNFFT is the counterpart of FFTW's brfft/rfft:\n\nhatf_j = operatornameRe sum_bmn h_bmn  mathrme^-2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j\nqquad\nh_bmn = sum_j=1^J y_j  mathrme^2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j quad text(adjoint)\n\nadjoint to each other with respect to the real inner product. It computes on a real oversampled grid, which saves about half of the FFT and of the adjoint resampling; see Real Data.\n\nCosine and sine transforms (NFCT, NFST). The non-equidistant fast cosine and sine transforms of NFFT3 use real data and nodes in 0 12^D:\n\nhatf_j = sum_bmn f_bmn prod_d=1^D cos(2pi n_d k_dj)\nqquad\nhatf_j = sum_bmn f_bmn prod_d=1^D sin(2pi n_d k_dj)\n\nwith n_d = 0dotsN_d-1 (cosine) and n_d = 1dotsN_d (sine) for an array of size bmN. Their transposes take the role of the adjoint. NFFT.jl computes them as NFFTs of the even and odd extension of the data, see Cosine and Sine Transforms.\n\nNon-equidistant on both sides (NNFFT). For arbitrary real sources bmy_l and targets bmk_j,\n\nhatf_j = sum_l f_l  mathrme^-2pimathrmibmk_jcdotbmy_l\n\nThe sources are spread with a window onto an equidistant grid, an NFFT evaluates that grid at the scaled targets, and a division by the Fourier transform of the window undoes the spreading; see Non-equidistant in Both Domains.","title":"Other transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#Accuracy-and-Parameters","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"The NFFT approximates the NDFT; its error depends on the window function varphi, the window width W (in grid points) and the oversampling factor sigma (see Mathematical Background). NFFT.jl lets you state the error you need and chooses these three for you.","title":"Accuracy and Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#From-reltol-to-parameters","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"reltol is the requested relative error in the ell^2 norm, for random data. For each combination of window family, W and sigma NFFT.jl carries a table with the optimised shape parameter of the window and the error it reaches. From all entries whose predicted error is below reltol, the plan takes the one with the lowest predicted run time. The cost model weighs the resampling (J W^D) against the FFT (sigma^D bmN log), so the choice depends on the dimension and on the ratio of nodes to grid size. Plans on a GPU use a cost model of their own (see Parameter choice on a GPU).\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nN = (48, 48)\nk = rand(2, 4000) .- 0.5\nf = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nexact = NDFTPlan(k, N) * f\nrelerr(p) = norm(p * f - exact) / norm(exact)\n\np = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-6)\nNFFTPlan{Float64} of size (48, 48) along dims 1:2 at 4000 nodes: ExpSemicircle window, W = 7, σ = 2.0, predicted error 9.97192883345134e-7\n\nThe plan shows what it chose; NFFT.parameters returns the same as a named tuple:\n\nNFFT.parameters(p)\n(window = ExpSemicircle(), W = 7, σ = 2.0)\n\nOver a range of tolerances, the measured error stays below the requested one, and accuracy(p) predicts it well:\n\ntols = [10.0^-i for i in 2:14]\nplans = [plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=t) for t in tols]\nmeasured = relerr.(plans)\npredicted = accuracy.(plans)\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xscale=log10, yscale=log10, xreversed=true,\n          xlabel=\"requested reltol\", ylabel=\"relative error\",\n          xticks=LogTicks(-14:2:-2), yticks=LogTicks(-14:2:-2))\nlines!(ax, tols, tols; color=:gray60, linestyle=:dash, label=\"reltol\")\nscatterlines!(ax, tols, predicted; label=\"accuracy(p)\")\nscatterlines!(ax, tols, measured; label=\"measured\")\naxislegend(ax; position=:rt)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe chosen widths and oversampling factors:\n\n[(reltol=t, W=NFFT.parameters(q).W, σ=NFFT.parameters(q).σ) for (t, q) in zip(tols, plans)]\n13-element Vector{@NamedTuple{reltol::Float64, W::Int64, σ::Float64}}:\n (reltol = 0.010000000000000002, W = 4, σ = 1.25)\n (reltol = 0.001, W = 5, σ = 1.4)\n (reltol = 0.0001, W = 6, σ = 1.45)\n (reltol = 1.0e-5, W = 7, σ = 1.5)\n (reltol = 1.0e-6, W = 7, σ = 2.0)\n (reltol = 1.0000000000000001e-7, W = 8, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-8, W = 9, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-9, W = 10, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-10, W = 11, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-11, W = 12, σ = 2.2)\n (reltol = 1.0000000000000002e-12, W = 13, σ = 2.2)\n (reltol = 1.0e-13, W = 14, σ = 2.2)\n (reltol = 1.0e-14, W = 15, σ = 2.5)\n\nBelow about 10^-14 rounding errors dominate in double precision; in single precision the floor is about 10^-6. A reltol below the floor gives the most accurate plan available.","title":"From reltol to parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#Window-families","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"Two families have parameter tables:\n\nKaiserBessel: the continuous Kaiser–Bessel window,\nExpSemicircle: the \"exponential of semicircle\" window exp(beta((1-(2zW)^2)^e_0 - 1)).\n\nThe default window = :auto lets both compete. How the error falls with the width, at sigma = 2 and sigma = 125:\n\nWs = 4:14\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; yscale=log10, xlabel=\"window width W\", ylabel=\"relative error\",\n          xticks=Ws, yticks=LogTicks(-14:2:-2))\nfor (σ, style) in ((2.0, :solid), (1.25, :dash)), (win, name) in ((KaiserBessel(), \"Kaiser–Bessel\"),\n                                                                   (ExpSemicircle(), \"ES\"))\n  errs = [relerr(plan_nfft(k, N; window=win, W, σ)) for W in Ws]\n  scatterlines!(ax, Ws, errs; linestyle=style, label=\"$name, σ = $σ\")\nend\naxislegend(ax; position=:lb)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA smaller sigma needs a wider window for the same error, but its FFT is cheaper; which is faster overall depends on D and on the number of nodes. This is the trade-off the automatic choice makes.\n\nTo set window, width or oversampling yourself, see Choosing Parameters; all plan options are listed in the API.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Window families"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/nnfft/#Non-equidistant-in-Both-Domains","page":"Non-equidistant in Both Domains","text":"This guide shows how to compute the NNFFT (type 3 in the terminology of FINUFFT), which has arbitrary real points in both domains: values f_l at M sources bmy_l in mathbbR^D are transformed to J targets bmk_j in mathbbR^D,\n\nhatf_j = sum_l=1^M f_l  mathrme^-2pimathrmibmk_jcdotbmy_l\nqquad\nf_l = sum_j=1^J hatf_j  mathrme^2pimathrmibmk_jcdotbmy_l quad text(adjoint)\n\nNeither the sources nor the targets need to lie in a fixed interval. plan_nnfft(k, y) takes the targets k (D times J) and the sources y (D times M) and the options listed in the API. The cost grows with the extent of the two point sets, not with their number (see How NFFT.jl Works): wide spreads in both domains make the transform expensive.","title":"Non-equidistant in Both Domains"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/nnfft/#Fourier-transform-of-a-function-sampled-at-random-points","page":"Non-equidistant in Both Domains","text":"A Gaussian f(y) = mathrme^-pi y^2 has the Fourier transform hatf(k) = int_-infty^infty f(y) mathrme^-2pimathrmiky mathrmdy = mathrme^-pi k^2. With samples at random points and trapezoidal weights, the integral becomes an NNFFT, evaluated here at Chebyshev points in -3 3:\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\ny = sort(12 .* rand(4000) .- 6)                 # sources in [-6, 6]\nw = [y[2] - y[1]; (y[3:end] .- y[1:end-2]) ./ 2; y[end] - y[end-1]]\nk = 3 .* cospi.((2 .* (1:300) .- 1) ./ 600)    # targets in [-3, 3]\n\np = plan_nnfft(reshape(k, 1, :), reshape(y, 1, :); reltol=1e-10)\nF = p * complex.(w .* exp.(-π .* y .^ 2))\nmaximum(abs, F .- exp.(-π .* k .^ 2))\n0.00011047429389550023\n\nThe remaining difference is the error of the trapezoidal rule on the random points, not that of the NNFFT. Against the direct sum:\n\npd = NNDFTPlan(reshape(k, 1, :), reshape(y, 1, :))\nnorm(F - pd * complex.(w .* exp.(-π .* y .^ 2))) / norm(F), accuracy(p)\n(2.1252466301425646e-12, 9.809040167511179e-11)\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"k\", ylabel=\"Re F(k)\")\nlines!(ax, -3:0.01:3, k -> exp(-π * k^2); color=:gray, label=\"exp(-πk²)\")\nscatter!(ax, k, real.(F); markersize=5, label=\"NNFFT\")\naxislegend(ax)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Fourier transform of a function sampled at random points"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/nnfft/#Several-dimensions","page":"Non-equidistant in Both Domains","text":"Sources and targets in 2D, off-centre on purpose, against the direct sum:\n\nys = 40 .* rand(2, 5000) .+ [100.0, -20.0]\nks = 2 .* rand(2, 3000) .- [0.3, 1.5]\nf = randn(ComplexF64, 5000)\np2 = plan_nnfft(ks, ys; reltol=1e-8)\nnorm(p2 * f - NNDFTPlan(ks, ys) * f) / norm(p2 * f), accuracy(p2)\n(2.1199118198691953e-9, 8.9788430791458e-9)\n\nThe plan reports the size of the inner NFFT and the window of the spreading:\n\np2\nNNFFTPlan{Float64} from 5000 sources to 3000 targets: inner NFFT of size (140, 140), spreading with ExpSemicircle W = 11, σ = 1.55, predicted error 8.9788430791458e-9\n\nThe adjoint is the transform from the targets back to the sources:\n\nv = randn(ComplexF64, 3000)\nnorm(adjoint(p2) * v - adjoint(NNDFTPlan(ks, ys)) * v) / norm(adjoint(p2) * v)\n2.141559925099786e-9","title":"Several dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/nnfft/#Many-signals-at-the-same-points","page":"Non-equidistant in Both Domains","text":"With the size of the data array as third argument, dims selects the dimension that holds the sources; the other dimensions are batch dimensions. Eight signals at the 5000 sources:\n\npb = plan_nnfft(ks, ys, (5000, 8); dims=1, reltol=1e-8)\nF = randn(ComplexF64, 5000, 8)\nsize(pb * F), pb * F ≈ reduce(hcat, [p2 * F[:, c] for c in 1:8])\n((3000, 8), true)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Many signals at the same points"},{"category":"section","location":"#NFFT.jl","page":"Home","text":"Fast Fourier transforms at non-equidistant nodes: a Julia package for CPUs and GPUs, and a library for Python and C compiled from the same code.\n\nwarning: Development version\nThis documentation describes NFFT.jl 1.0, which is under development on the main branch and not released yet. Pkg.add(\"NFFT\") installs version 0.14, documented here. See Migrating from NFFT.jl 0.x.\n\nNFFT.jl computes the non-equidistant fast Fourier transform (NFFT, also called NUFFT, or gridding in MRI): a discrete Fourier transform where the nodes in one domain do not lie on a grid. It is accurate to a tolerance you choose and runs in mathcalO(bmN log bmN + J) operations for J nodes and an array of size bmN. The same plans run multi-threaded on the CPU and on GPUs (CUDA, AMD, Apple).","title":"NFFT.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Julia\n\nusing NFFT\n\nk = rand(2, 10_000) .- 0.5           # 10 000 nodes in [-1/2, 1/2)²\nf = randn(ComplexF64, 256, 256)      # equidistant data\n\np = plan_nfft(k, size(f))            # relative error 1e-8 by default\nfHat = p * f                         # NFFT: equidistant → nodes\ng = adjoint(p) * fHat                # adjoint NFFT: nodes → equidistant\n\nPython (experimental, see Python)\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport nfftjl\n\nk = np.random.rand(10_000, 2) - 0.5               # 10 000 nodes in [-1/2, 1/2)², one per row\nf = np.random.randn(256, 256) + 0j                # equidistant data\n\np = nfftjl.Plan(k, f.shape)                       # relative error 1e-8 by default\nfhat = p.trafo(f)                                 # NFFT: equidistant → nodes\ng = p.adjoint(fhat)                               # adjoint NFFT: nodes → equidistant\n\nC (experimental, see C)\n\n#include \"nfftjl.h\"\n\nint64_t N[] = {256, 256};             /* column major: node j is k[2j], k[2j+1] */\nint64_t p = nfftjl_plan_create(2, N, J, k, 1e-8, nfftjl_num_threads());\nnfftjl_trafo(p, f, fhat);             /* NFFT: equidistant → nodes */\nnfftjl_adjoint(p, fhat, g);           /* adjoint NFFT: nodes → equidistant */\nnfftjl_plan_destroy(p);","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-is-available-in-which-language","page":"Home","text":"The Julia package is the full library. Python and C use libnfftjl, a library compiled from the same Julia code with juliac --trim: no Julia installation is needed to use it, and it is as fast as the Julia package. Its interface is experimental and covers the core transform so far.\n\n Julia Python C\nNFFT and adjoint, reltol chooses the parameters ✓ ✓ ✓\ndimensions any 1–3 1–3\nprecision Float32, Float64 complex64, complex128 float, double\nmulti-threaded ✓ ✓ ✓\ntransforms along dimensions (batches) ✓ ✓ ✓\nRNFFT, NFCT, NFST ✓ ✓ ✓\nNNFFT ✓ ✓ ✓\nGPUs ✓ – –\nstages, density compensation, Toeplitz ✓ – –\nstatus 1.0 (in development) experimental experimental","title":"What is available in which language"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-NFFT.jl-offers","page":"Home","text":"You ask for an accuracy, not for parameters. reltol is all you need to set. The plan picks the window function, its width and the oversampling factor that reach this error at the lowest predicted cost, and accuracy(p) tells you what it expects to achieve.\nCPU and GPU from one code base. Every transform, including batch dimensions, runs on the GPU when the plan gets a GPU array type: plan_nfft(k, N; storage=CuArray). The GPU kernels are written with KernelAbstractions.jl and serve CUDA, AMD and Apple GPUs alike; see GPU.\nFast. Tiled resampling with piecewise-polynomial window evaluation, an FFT that skips the zero padding, and multi-threading in every stage. See Performance for a comparison with FINUFFT and ducc.\nTransforms along some dimensions of an array (dims), with batch kernels that process many transforms with the same nodes at once, as needed for multi-coil MRI or OCT.\nReal data at the nodes (plan_rnfft, e.g. OCT), on a real grid.\nCosine and sine transforms (NFCT, NFST) for real data, with the definitions of NFFT3 and about the cost of a complex NFFT.\nNon-equidistant points in both domains (NNFFT, plan_nnfft).\nSingle and double precision, with node positions always computed in double precision.\nThe three stages of the algorithm as an interface: resampling correction, FFT and resampling can be called one by one, for example to embed the NFFT into a Toeplitz approach or to profile it.\nAutomatic differentiation with Zygote (ChainRules) and Enzyme: a plan is a constant linear map, its derivative is the plan itself or its adjoint.\nOne interface for several implementations. AbstractNFFTs.jl defines plans, options and operations; NFFT.jl and NonuniformFFTs.jl implement it. Code written against the interface runs with each of them.","title":"What NFFT.jl offers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"NFFT\")\n\nThis also installs AbstractNFFTs.jl, the interface package. Sampling density compensation and Toeplitz kernels are in NFFTTools.jl (Pkg.add(\"NFFTTools\")). For Python and C see Python and C.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Guide","page":"Home","text":"The documentation follows the Diátaxis structure:\n\nGetting started, to learn NFFT.jl step by step:\n\nGetting Started in Julia: plans, transforms, precision, threads and changing nodes.\nPython and C: installation, a first transform, the interface, threads.\n\nHow-to guides (Julia), for a task at hand:\n\nTransforms along Dimensions: dims and many transforms with the same nodes.\nReal Data: real values at the nodes with plan_rnfft (e.g. OCT).\nCosine and Sine Transforms: the NFCT and NFST.\nNon-equidistant in Both Domains: the NNFFT.\nGPU: all transforms on GPUs.\nDensity Compensation and Toeplitz Kernels: NFFTTools.jl.\nChoosing Parameters: window, width, oversampling and FFT planning by hand.\nStages of the NFFT: calling the stages one by one.\nMigrating from NFFT.jl 0.x: what changed in version 1.0.\n\nExplanation, for understanding:\n\nMathematical Background: the transforms and the three stages of the algorithm.\nAccuracy and Parameters: how reltol becomes window, width and oversampling.\nHow NFFT.jl Works: tiles, halo, pruned FFT, batches, real data, NNFFT and GPUs, with the ideas behind them.\nPerformance: NFFT.jl against FINUFFT, ducc and NonuniformFFTs.jl.\n\nReference, to look things up:\n\nAPI: all exported functions and types.\nAbstract Interface: AbstractNFFTs.jl and how to write an implementation.","title":"Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"The source code is licensed under the MIT license. If you use NFFT.jl in your work, please cite\n\nT. Knopp, M. Boberg and M. Grosser, NFFT.jl: Generic and Fast Julia Implementation of the Nonequidistant Fast Fourier Transform, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 45(3), C179–C205, 2023 (arXiv:2208.00049).\n\nA BibTeX entry is in NFFT.bib in the root of the repository.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contact","page":"Home","text":"Bugs and ideas go to the issue tracker, questions to the discussions.","title":"Contact"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contributors","page":"Home","text":"Tobias Knopp\nNiklas Hackelberg\nRobert Dahl Jacobsen\nMirco Grosser\nJakob Assländer\nMosè Giordano\nJeff Fessler\n\nThe complete list is on GitHub.","title":"Contributors"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Python","page":"Python","text":"note: Experimental\nThe Python package nfftjl wraps libnfftjl, NFFT.jl compiled into a library with juliac --trim (see C). The wheel carries the library and its Julia runtime; no Julia installation is needed to use it. It covers the NFFT, RNFFT, NFCT, NFST and NNFFT and their adjoints in 1–3 dimensions, with batches, in single and double precision; the table on the home page lists what the Julia package offers beyond that. The interface may still change.","title":"Python"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Installation","page":"Python","text":"nfftjl is not on PyPI yet. A wheel for your platform is built from the repository (building needs Julia ≥ 1.12, using the wheel does not):\n\ngit clone https://github.com/JuliaMath/NFFT.jl && cd NFFT.jl\nlibnfftjl/build.sh                                  # the library, ≈ 2 min\npython3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install numpy build wheel setuptools\nPYTHON=.venv/bin/python bindings/python/build_wheel.sh\n.venv/bin/pip install bindings/python/dist/*.whl","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#A-first-transform","page":"Python","text":"The NFFT evaluates hat f_j = sum_bm l f_bm l  e^-2pi i bm l cdot bm k_j at nodes bm k_j in -12 12)^D for the equidistant array f (see Mathematical Background); the adjoint is the sum with the opposite sign. This example computes both and checks the first values against the direct sum:\n\n# A 2D NFFT and its adjoint with nfftjl, checked against the direct sum.\nimport numpy as np\nimport nfftjl\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN = (64, 48)                                      # size of the equidistant array\nk = rng.random((1000, 2)) - 0.5                   # 1000 nodes in [-1/2, 1/2)², one per row\nf = rng.standard_normal(N) + 1j * rng.standard_normal(N)\n\np = nfftjl.Plan(k, N, reltol=1e-9)                # nthreads: all threads of the library\nfhat = p.trafo(f)                                 # fhat_j = Σ_l f_l exp(-2πi l·k_j)\ng = p.adjoint(fhat)                               # g_l = Σ_j fhat_j exp(+2πi l·k_j)\n\n# the direct sum at the first ten nodes; l_d = -N_d//2, …, N_d - N_d//2 - 1 along axis d of f\ngrid = np.meshgrid(*[np.arange(-(n // 2), n - n // 2) for n in N], indexing=\"ij\")\nl = np.stack(grid, axis=-1)                       # shape N + (2,)\nref = np.array([np.sum(f * np.exp(-2j * np.pi * (l @ kj))) for kj in k[:10]])\nerr = np.linalg.norm(fhat[:10] - ref) / np.linalg.norm(ref)\nprint(f\"relative error {err:.1e}\")\nassert err < 1e-8","title":"A first transform"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Interface","page":"Python","text":"nfftjl.Plan(k, shape, reltol=1e-8, nthreads=None, dtype=numpy.complex128, axes=None) : Plan for the nodes k, an array of shape (J, D) (one node per row; shape (J,) in 1D) with   entries in -12 12), and data of shape shape (an integer or a tuple), transformed along   axes: all axes by default, otherwise D = 1, 2 or 3 consecutive axes (column d of k   belongs to axes[d]). The other axes are batch axes, see Batches. reltol is the   requested relative error: window, window width and oversampling factor are chosen for it at the   lowest predicted cost (see Accuracy and Parameters).   nthreads defaults to num_threads(). dtype is the element type of the data,   numpy.complex128 or numpy.complex64; the nodes are given in double precision either way, and   grid positions are computed from them in double precision.\n\np.trafo(f, out=None) : The NFFT of f (shape p.shape, converted to dtype): an array of shape p.out_shape, the   transformed axes replaced by one axis of length J.\n\np.adjoint(fhat, out=None) : The adjoint NFFT of fhat (shape p.out_shape): an array of shape p.shape.\n\np.close() : Releases the plan; garbage collection does the same.\n\nnfftjl.num_threads() : The default number of threads of a plan: the environment variable NFFTJL_NUM_THREADS if set,   otherwise the number of performance cores (Apple Silicon) or of all CPU threads. The library   has one thread per CPU thread, the upper limit of nthreads.\n\nAlong axis d of f, index i stands for the frequency l_d = i - N_d2 (N_d2 rounded down), in increasing order, as numpy.fft.fftshift orders them. Arrays in numpy's default (C) order are passed to the library without a copy. With out the result goes into an existing array.\n\nThe transform equals FINUFFT's type 2 with x = 2π k, isign = -1 and the default mode ordering; the adjoint equals type 1 with isign = +1.","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Real-data:-RNFFT,-NFCT,-NFST","page":"Python","text":"nfftjl.RNFFTPlan, nfftjl.NFCTPlan and nfftjl.NFSTPlan take the same arguments as Plan (dtype is the real type, numpy.float64 or numpy.float32); p.in_shape and p.out_shape give the shapes of their data:\n\nRNFFT (real values at the nodes): hat f_j = mathrmRe sum_bm n h_bm n e^-2pi i bm n cdot bm k_j with n = 0 dots N2 along the last transformed axis (the layout of numpy.fft.rfftn) and as for the NFFT along the others; h is complex, the values at the nodes are real. The adjoint is h_bm n = sum_j hat f_j e^2pi i bm n cdot bm k_j. Nodes in -12 12).\nNFCT and NFST: hat f_j = sum_bm n f_bm n prod_d cos(2pi n_d k_dj) over n_d = 0 dots N_d - 1, or with sin over n_d = 1 dots N_d, for real f; adjoint is the transpose. Nodes in 0 12.\n\n# The transforms for real data: an NFCT in 2D checked against its direct sum, and an RNFFT whose\n# coefficients have the layout of numpy.fft.rfftn.\nimport numpy as np\nimport nfftjl\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(2)\nN = (32, 24)\nk = rng.random((500, 2)) / 2                       # NFCT/NFST nodes in [0, 1/2]\nf = rng.standard_normal(N)\n\np = nfftjl.NFCTPlan(k, N, reltol=1e-9)\nfhat = p.trafo(f)                                  # sum_n f_n cos(2π n_1 k_1) cos(2π n_2 k_2)\nn1, n2 = np.meshgrid(np.arange(N[0]), np.arange(N[1]), indexing=\"ij\")\nref = np.array([np.sum(f * np.cos(2 * np.pi * n1 * x) * np.cos(2 * np.pi * n2 * y)) for x, y in k])\nerr = np.linalg.norm(fhat - ref) / np.linalg.norm(ref)\nprint(f\"NFCT: relative error {err:.1e}\")\nassert err < 1e-8\n\nq = nfftjl.RNFFTPlan(rng.random((500, 2)) - 0.5, N)\nprint(\"RNFFT: coefficients\", q.in_shape, \"complex, values at the nodes\", q.out_shape, \"real\")\nassert q.in_shape == (32, 13) and q.trafo(np.ones(q.in_shape, dtype=complex)).dtype == np.float64\n\nA plan whose nodes lie outside the range of its transform raises a ValueError with the reason.","title":"Real data: RNFFT, NFCT, NFST"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Non-equidistant-in-both-domains:-NNFFT","page":"Python","text":"nfftjl.NNFFTPlan(k, y, shape=None, reltol=1e-8, nthreads=None, dtype=numpy.complex128, axis=-1) computes hat f_j = sum_l f_l e^-2pi i bm k_j cdot bm y_l for targets k (shape (J, D)) and sources y (shape (N, D)) with arbitrary real coordinates, and the adjoint. The cost grows with the product of the extents of k and y, not with J and N. With shape, the data have batch axes around axis, the axis of the sources, which the transform replaces by the J targets. It equals FINUFFT's type 3 with x = 2π y, s = k, isign = -1.\n\n# NNFFT: sums of exponentials at arbitrary real frequencies and positions, here a 2D signal of\n# point sources sampled at scattered frequencies, checked against the direct sum.\nimport numpy as np\nimport nfftjl\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(3)\ny = 50 * rng.random((2000, 2))                     # source positions\nk = 3 * rng.random((5000, 2)) - 1.5                # frequencies\nf = rng.standard_normal(2000) + 1j * rng.standard_normal(2000)\n\np = nfftjl.NNFFTPlan(k, y, reltol=1e-9)\nfhat = p.trafo(f)                                  # sum_l f_l exp(-2πi k_j·y_l)\nref = np.exp(-2j * np.pi * k[:100] @ y.T) @ f\nerr = np.linalg.norm(fhat[:100] - ref) / np.linalg.norm(ref)\nprint(f\"NNFFT: {len(f)} sources, {len(fhat)} targets, relative error {err:.1e}\")\nassert err < 1e-8","title":"Non-equidistant in both domains: NNFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Batches","page":"Python","text":"Every slice along the batch axes is transformed with the same nodes, for example the coil images in MRI. One plan for all slices is faster than a loop over slices: the nodes are binned once, and the batch kernels of NFFT.jl process several slices per pass over the nodes.\n\n# Many transforms with the same nodes: eight coil images of 128 × 128, transformed along axes 1 and 2\n# with one plan, compared with one plan per coil.\nimport numpy as np\nimport nfftjl\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(1)\nk = rng.random((20_000, 2)) - 0.5                  # nodes, one per row\nimages = rng.standard_normal((8, 128, 128)) + 0j   # coil × x × y\n\np = nfftjl.Plan(k, images.shape, axes=(1, 2))      # axis 0 is a batch axis\ndata = p.trafo(images)                             # shape (8, 20000)\nback = p.adjoint(data)                             # shape (8, 128, 128)\n\nsingle = nfftjl.Plan(k, (128, 128))\nref = np.stack([single.trafo(images[c]) for c in range(8)])\nerr = np.linalg.norm(data - ref) / np.linalg.norm(ref)\nprint(f\"shape {data.shape}, difference to single plans {err:.1e}\")\nassert data.shape == (8, 20_000) and back.shape == images.shape and err < 1e-12","title":"Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Threads","page":"Python","text":"One plan must not be executed by two threads at the same time: create one plan per thread. Separate plans run concurrently, each with its own nthreads.\nThe default number of threads of a plan is the environment variable NFFTJL_NUM_THREADS if set, otherwise the number of performance cores on Apple Silicon and of all CPU threads elsewhere. A 1D transform runs its FFT in one piece per thread, so a thread on an efficiency core or on a core busy with other work holds it up (up to 2× slower with all 10 threads of an M1 Max); on a busy machine fewer threads than cores are faster.\nnumpy's OpenBLAS threads keep spinning for a while after a numpy call and slow down a transform that follows; OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 avoids this when timing.","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Comparison-with-FINUFFT","page":"Python","text":"bindings/python/tests/bench.py compares nfftjl with FINUFFT's Python package on the problems of the Performance page. The library runs the same machine code as the Julia package.","title":"Comparison with FINUFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Migrating-from-NFFT.jl-0.x","page":"Migrating from NFFT.jl 0.x","text":"This guide shows how to update code written for NFFT.jl 0.x. Version 1.0 is a new implementation with a cleaner interface; most code that only uses plan_nfft, *, mul!, adjoint and reltol runs unchanged. The changes:\n\nNFFT.jl 0.x NFFT.jl 1.0\nm, σ W = 2m (window width; odd widths allowed), σ\nwindow = :kaiser_bessel, … window = KaiserBessel(), ExpSemicircle(), CustomWindow(φ); default :auto\nprecompute = POLYNOMIAL / TENSOR / LINEAR / FULL removed; the window is always evaluated as a piecewise polynomial\nblocking, sortNodes, blockSize removed (always tiled); tilesize as an expert option\nstoreDeconvolutionIdx, LUTSize removed\nfftflags = FFTW.MEASURE fft = FFTWBackend(flags=FFTW.MEASURE)\ntiming keyword, TimingStats removed; time the stages instead\npositional array type plan_nfft(Array, k, N) storage = Array\nusing CuNFFT, plan_nfft(CuArray, k, N) using CUDA, plan_nfft(k, N; storage=CuArray)\nNFFT._use_threads[] = false nthreads = 1\nconvolve!, convolve_transpose! resampling!, resampling_adjoint!\ndeconvolve!, deconvolve_transpose! resampling_correction!, resampling_correction_adjoint!\np.Ñ oversampled_size(p)\n\nBehaviour that changed:\n\nUnknown options are errors. In 0.x, misspelled or unsupported keywords could be ignored silently.\nreltol is met. The plan chooses window, width and oversampling from tables of measured errors; accuracy(p) reports the predicted error.\nNodes are checked. Nodes outside -12 12 or not finite throw an ArgumentError.\ndims accepts contiguous ranges of dimensions, not only a single dimension, and uses batch kernels.\nGPU plans come from storage (or GPU nodes) instead of a positional array type and need no extra package besides the GPU package; see GPU.","title":"Migrating from NFFT.jl 0.x"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Stages-of-the-NFFT","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"This guide shows how to call the three stages of the NFFT one by one: resampling correction, FFT and resampling (see Mathematical Background). The stage interface works on the oversampled array of size oversampled_size(p). It is useful for methods that operate on the oversampled grid, such as Toeplitz embeddings, for teaching, and for profiling.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nN = (256, 256)\nk = rand(2, 100_000) .- 0.5\np = plan_nfft(k, N)\nÑ = oversampled_size(p)\n(432, 432)","title":"Stages of the NFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Run-the-direct-NFFT-stage-by-stage","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"f = randn(ComplexF64, N)\ng = zeros(ComplexF64, Ñ)\nfHat = zeros(ComplexF64, size(k, 2))\n\nresampling_correction!(p, f, g)   # D: divide by φ̂, zero-pad\noversampled_fft!(p, g)            # F\nresampling!(p, g, fHat)           # B: evaluate at the nodes\nfHat ≈ p * f\ntrue","title":"Run the direct NFFT stage by stage"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Run-the-adjoint-stage-by-stage","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"The adjoint runs the adjoint stages in reverse order:\n\nback = zeros(ComplexF64, N)\nresampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g)           # Bᴴ: add node values to the grid\noversampled_bfft!(p, g)                   # Fᴴ\nresampling_correction_adjoint!(p, g, back) # Dᴴ: crop, divide by φ̂\nback ≈ adjoint(p) * fHat\ntrue","title":"Run the adjoint stage by stage"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Find-out-where-the-time-goes","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"Timing the stages shows which part dominates. The FFT stages of this interface transform the full oversampled array, while p * f skips the zero padding, so the sum is a bit larger than a complete transform.\n\ntmin(op) = (op(); minimum(@elapsed(op()) for _ in 1:5))\nt = [tmin(() -> resampling_correction!(p, f, g)),\n     tmin(() -> oversampled_fft!(p, g)),\n     tmin(() -> resampling!(p, g, fHat)),\n     tmin(() -> resampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g)),\n     tmin(() -> oversampled_bfft!(p, g)),\n     tmin(() -> resampling_correction_adjoint!(p, g, back))]\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"time / ms\", yticks=(1:2, [\"NFFT\", \"adjoint\"]))\ncolors = Makie.wong_colors()\nnames = [\"resampling correction\", \"FFT\", \"resampling\"]\nfor (row, idx) in ((1, 1:3), (2, [6, 5, 4]))\n  left = 0.0\n  for (c, i) in enumerate(idx)\n    barplot!(ax, [row], [1e3t[i]]; direction=:x, offset=left, fillto=left, color=colors[c])\n    left += 1e3t[i]\n  end\nend\nLegend(fig[2, 1], [PolyElement(color=colors[c]) for c in 1:3], names;\n       orientation=:horizontal, framevisible=false)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe stage interface needs a plan without batch dimensions (dims covering all dimensions). NFCT and NFST plans have it as well: their oversampled array holds the stored grid points, and the FFT stages are the DCT-I/DST-I and its transpose.","title":"Find out where the time goes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Real-values-at-the-nodes","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"For an RNFFT plan the oversampled array is real, and the resampling correction writes the complex half spectrum of size spectrum_size(p). The FFT stages map one into the other and therefore only exist out of place: oversampled_fft!(p, ĝ, g) and oversampled_bfft!(p, g, ĝ). The other plans accept this form as well.\n\npr = plan_rnfft(k, N)\nh = randn(ComplexF64, size_in(pr))\nĝ = zeros(ComplexF64, spectrum_size(pr))\ngr = zeros(oversampled_size(pr))\ny = zeros(size(k, 2))\nresampling_correction!(pr, h, ĝ)\noversampled_fft!(pr, ĝ, gr)\nresampling!(pr, gr, y)\ny ≈ pr * h\ntrue","title":"Real values at the nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#The-resampling-as-a-matrix","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"For analysis and teaching, sparse(p) (with using SparseArrays) returns the resampling stage as a sparse matrix bmB with W^D window weights per node; the resampling is hatbm f = bmBmathrmvec(bm g) and its adjoint bmB^mathsf H. The transforms themselves never build it.\n\nusing SparseArrays\nB = sparse(p)\nsize(B), nnz(B), B * vec(g) ≈ resampling!(p, g, similar(fHat))\n((100000, 186624), 10000000, true)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"The resampling as a matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"c/#C","page":"C","text":"note: Experimental\nlibnfftjl is NFFT.jl compiled into a shared library with juliac --trim. It carries its Julia runtime; programs that use it need no Julia installation. It covers the NFFT, RNFFT, NFCT, NFST and NNFFT and their adjoints in 1–3 dimensions, with batches, in single and double precision (functions nfftjl_rnfft_…, nfftjl_nfct_…, nfftjl_nfst_…, nfftjl_nnfft_…, see the header below); the table on the home page lists what the Julia package offers beyond that. The interface may still change.","title":"C"},{"category":"section","location":"c/#Building","page":"C","text":"Building needs Julia ≥ 1.12:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/JuliaMath/NFFT.jl && cd NFFT.jl\nlibnfftjl/build.sh\n\nThe result is libnfftjl/build/: lib/libnfftjl.so (.dylib on macOS) next to the Julia runtime it needs, and the artifacts (FFTW) in share/. Keep the directory together. A program compiles against the header libnfftjl/include/nfftjl.h:\n\ncc -O2 prog.c -Ilibnfftjl/include -Llibnfftjl/build/lib -lnfftjl -lm -Wl,-rpath,$PWD/libnfftjl/build/lib\n\nThe library starts one Julia thread per CPU thread; a plan uses nthreads of them, by default the value of the environment variable NFFTJL_NUM_THREADS or one per performance core. It is built with Julia's signal handling, which the garbage collector needs to stop threads, and for one CPU target (see libnfftjl/README.md).","title":"Building"},{"category":"section","location":"c/#A-first-transform","page":"C","text":"The NFFT evaluates hat f_j = sum_bm l f_bm l  e^-2pi i bm l cdot bm k_j at nodes bm k_j in -12 12)^D for the equidistant array f (see Mathematical Background); the adjoint is the sum with the opposite sign. This program computes both and checks the first values against the direct sum:\n\n/* A 2D NFFT and its adjoint with libnfftjl, checked against the direct sum.\n   cc -O2 getting_started.c -I../include -L../build/lib -lnfftjl -lm -Wl,-rpath,../build/lib */\n#include <complex.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include \"nfftjl.h\"\n\nint main(void) {\n  const int D = 2;\n  const int64_t N[] = {64, 48}, J = 1000;     /* array size, number of nodes */\n  const int64_t M = N[0] * N[1];\n\n  /* column major: node j is k[2j], k[2j+1]; f[l0 + N0*l1] */\n  double *k = malloc(sizeof(double) * D * J);\n  double complex *f = malloc(sizeof(double complex) * M), *fhat = malloc(sizeof(double complex) * J),\n                 *g = malloc(sizeof(double complex) * M);\n  srand(0);\n  for (int64_t i = 0; i < D * J; i++) k[i] = rand() / (RAND_MAX + 1.0) - 0.5;\n  for (int64_t i = 0; i < M; i++) f[i] = rand() / (RAND_MAX + 1.0) - 0.5;\n\n  int64_t p = nfftjl_plan_create(D, N, J, k, 1e-9, nfftjl_num_threads());\n  if (p < 0) return 1;\n  nfftjl_trafo(p, f, fhat);                   /* fhat_j = Σ_l f_l exp(-2πi l·k_j) */\n  nfftjl_adjoint(p, fhat, g);                 /* g_l = Σ_j fhat_j exp(+2πi l·k_j) */\n\n  /* the direct sum at the first ten nodes; l_d = -N_d/2, …, N_d - N_d/2 - 1 */\n  double e = 0, s = 0;\n  for (int64_t j = 0; j < 10; j++) {\n    double complex ref = 0;\n    for (int64_t l1 = 0; l1 < N[1]; l1++)\n      for (int64_t l0 = 0; l0 < N[0]; l0++)\n        ref += f[l0 + N[0] * l1] *\n               cexp(-2 * M_PI * I * ((l0 - N[0] / 2) * k[D * j] + (l1 - N[1] / 2) * k[D * j + 1]));\n    e += pow(cabs(fhat[j] - ref), 2); s += pow(cabs(ref), 2);\n  }\n  printf(\"relative error %.1e\\n\", sqrt(e / s));\n\n  nfftjl_plan_destroy(p);\n  free(k); free(f); free(fhat); free(g);\n  return sqrt(e / s) < 1e-8 ? 0 : 1;\n}","title":"A first transform"},{"category":"section","location":"c/#Interface","page":"C","text":"/* NFFT.jl as a C library (built with juliac --trim). Arrays are column major (Julia order):\n   nodes k[d + D*j] in [-1/2, 1/2), coefficients f of size N[0] x ... x N[D-1],\n   fhat_j = sum_l f_l exp(-2 pi i l.k_j), l = -N/2 ... N-N/2-1 in every dimension. */\n#ifndef NFFTJL_H\n#define NFFTJL_H\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <complex.h>\n\n/* A plan for D = 1..3 dimensions, J nodes, relative error reltol, using nthreads threads\n   (0: the default, nfftjl_num_threads()); -1 on error. */\nint64_t nfftjl_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjl_trafo(int64_t plan, const double complex *f, double complex *fhat);\nint nfftjl_adjoint(int64_t plan, const double complex *fhat, double complex *f);\nint nfftjl_plan_destroy(int64_t plan);\n/* The message of the last failed plan creation, written into buf (len bytes, zero-terminated,\n   truncated); returns the length of the whole message. */\nsize_t nfftjl_last_error(char *buf, size_t len);\n\n/* A plan for many transforms with the same nodes: data of size L1 x N[0] x ... x N[D-1] x L3 and\n   L1 x J x L3 (column major), the transforms along the dimensions in between. */\nint64_t nfftjl_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                double reltol, int nthreads);\n\n/* RNFFT (real values at the nodes): fhat_j = Re sum_n h_n exp(-2 pi i n.k_j) over n_0 = 0 ... N[0]/2\n   and the other n_d as above; h has N[0]/2+1 x N[1] x ... complex values, fhat J real values.\n   The adjoint: h_n = sum_j fhat_j exp(2 pi i n.k_j). */\nint64_t nfftjl_rnfft_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjl_rnfft_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                      double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjl_rnfft_trafo(int64_t plan, const double complex *h, double *fhat);\nint nfftjl_rnfft_adjoint(int64_t plan, const double *fhat, double complex *h);\n\n/* NFCT and NFST (real data, nodes in [0, 1/2]): fhat_j = sum_n f_n prod_d cos(2 pi n_d k_dj) over\n   n_d = 0 ... N[d]-1 (NFCT), or with sin over n_d = 1 ... N[d] (NFST); the adjoint is the\n   transpose. */\nint64_t nfftjl_nfct_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjl_nfct_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                     double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjl_nfct_trafo(int64_t plan, const double *f, double *fhat);\nint nfftjl_nfct_adjoint(int64_t plan, const double *fhat, double *f);\nint64_t nfftjl_nfst_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjl_nfst_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                     double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjl_nfst_trafo(int64_t plan, const double *f, double *fhat);\nint nfftjl_nfst_adjoint(int64_t plan, const double *fhat, double *f);\n\n/* NNFFT (nonequispaced in both domains): fhat_j = sum_l f_l exp(-2 pi i k_j.y_l) for J targets\n   k[d + D*j] and N sources y[d + D*l], arbitrary real coordinates; the adjoint is\n   f_l = sum_j fhat_j exp(2 pi i k_j.y_l). The cost grows with the product of the extents of k and y,\n   not with J and N. _many: data of size L1 x N x L3 and L1 x J x L3. */\nint64_t nfftjl_nnfft_plan_create(int D, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t N, const double *y, double reltol,\n                                 int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjl_nnfft_plan_create_many(int D, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t N, const double *y, int64_t L1,\n                                      int64_t L3, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjl_nnfft_trafo(int64_t plan, const double complex *f, double complex *fhat);\nint nfftjl_nnfft_adjoint(int64_t plan, const double complex *fhat, double complex *f);\n/* Plans of all transforms are released by nfftjl_plan_destroy. */\n\n/* The same in single precision (data in float complex; nodes and reltol stay double). */\nint64_t nfftjlf_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjlf_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                 double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjlf_trafo(int64_t plan, const float complex *f, float complex *fhat);\nint nfftjlf_adjoint(int64_t plan, const float complex *fhat, float complex *f);\nint nfftjlf_plan_destroy(int64_t plan);\nint64_t nfftjlf_rnfft_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjlf_rnfft_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                       double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjlf_rnfft_trafo(int64_t plan, const float complex *h, float *fhat);\nint nfftjlf_rnfft_adjoint(int64_t plan, const float *fhat, float complex *h);\nint64_t nfftjlf_nfct_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjlf_nfct_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                      double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjlf_nfct_trafo(int64_t plan, const float *f, float *fhat);\nint nfftjlf_nfct_adjoint(int64_t plan, const float *fhat, float *f);\nint64_t nfftjlf_nfst_plan_create(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjlf_nfst_plan_create_many(int D, const int64_t *N, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t L1, int64_t L3,\n                                      double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjlf_nfst_trafo(int64_t plan, const float *f, float *fhat);\nint nfftjlf_nfst_adjoint(int64_t plan, const float *fhat, float *f);\nint64_t nfftjlf_nnfft_plan_create(int D, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t N, const double *y, double reltol,\n                                  int nthreads);\nint64_t nfftjlf_nnfft_plan_create_many(int D, int64_t J, const double *k, int64_t N, const double *y, int64_t L1,\n                                       int64_t L3, double reltol, int nthreads);\nint nfftjlf_nnfft_trafo(int64_t plan, const float complex *f, float complex *fhat);\nint nfftjlf_nnfft_adjoint(int64_t plan, const float complex *fhat, float complex *f);\n/* The default number of threads of a plan: the environment variable NFFTJL_NUM_THREADS if set,\n   otherwise the number of performance cores (Apple Silicon) or of all CPU threads (the library\n   starts one Julia thread per CPU thread, which is also the upper limit for nthreads). Plans can be created and\n   destroyed from any thread; one plan must not be executed on two threads at once. The Julia\n   runtime starts on the first call into the library: make that call from one thread before\n   others call concurrently (nfftjl_num_threads() will do). */\nint nfftjl_num_threads(void);\n\n#endif\n\nnfftjl_plan_create(D, N, J, k, reltol, nthreads) : A plan for D = 1, 2, 3 dimensions, the array size N[0..D-1] and J nodes k   (D times J, column major: node j is k[D*j], …, k[D*j + D-1], entries in   -12 12)). reltol is the requested relative error: window, window width and   oversampling factor are chosen for it at the lowest predicted cost (see   Accuracy and Parameters). nthreads = 0 uses nfftjl_num_threads(). Returns a handle,   or −1 on error. The nodes are copied.\n\nnfftjl_trafo(p, f, fhat), nfftjl_adjoint(p, fhat, f) : The NFFT of f (N_0 times dots times N_D-1 complex values, column major: element   (i_0 dots i_D-1) at i_0 + N_0 (i_1 + N_1 (i_2 dots))) into fhat (J values), and   the adjoint. Index i_d stands for the frequency l_d = i_d - lfloor N_d2 rfloor. Return   0, or −1 for an unknown handle.\n\nnfftjl_plan_create_many(D, N, J, k, L1, L3, reltol, nthreads) : A plan for many transforms with the same nodes: the data are L_1 times N_0 times dots times   N_D-1 times L_3 and L_1 times J times L_3 (column major), transformed along the   dimensions in between. nfftjl_plan_create is the case L_1 = L_3 = 1.\n\nnfftjl_plan_destroy(p) : Releases the plan.\n\nnfftjlf_plan_create, nfftjlf_plan_create_many, nfftjlf_trafo, nfftjlf_adjoint, nfftjlf_plan_destroy : The same in single precision: data in float complex; the nodes and reltol stay double, and   grid positions are computed from the nodes in double precision. A plan belongs to one precision:   the functions of the other return −1.\n\nnfftjl_nnfft_plan_create(D, J, k, N, y, reltol, nthreads), nfftjl_nnfft_plan_create_many(D, J, k, N, y, L1, L3, reltol, nthreads) : The NNFFT hat f_j = sum_l f_l e^-2pi i bm k_j cdot bm y_l from N sources y to J   targets k (both D times count, column major, arbitrary real coordinates); data of   L_1 times N times L_3 and L_1 times J times L_3 complex values. The cost grows with   the product of the extents of k and y, not with J and N (see   Non-equidistant in Both Domains). nfftjl_nnfft_trafo, nfftjl_nnfft_adjoint and the   nfftjlf_nnfft_… functions as for the NFFT.\n\nnfftjl_num_threads() : The default number of threads of a plan: NFFTJL_NUM_THREADS if set, otherwise the number of   performance cores (Apple Silicon) or of all CPU threads. The library has one thread per CPU   thread, the upper limit of nthreads.\n\nRow-major data of size N_0 times dots times N_D-1 is column-major data of the reversed size: pass the sizes and the coordinates of each node in reversed order (the Python package does this).","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"c/#Batches","page":"C","text":"One plan for many transforms with the same nodes is faster than a loop over single transforms: the nodes are binned once, and the batch kernels of NFFT.jl process several transforms per pass over the nodes.\n\n/* Many transforms with the same nodes: L = 8 images of 128 x 128, stored one after the other\n   (column major: 128 x 128 x 8, the batch after the transformed dimensions), with one plan,\n   compared with one plan per image. */\n#include <complex.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include \"nfftjl.h\"\n\nint main(void) {\n  const int D = 2;\n  const int64_t N[] = {128, 128}, J = 20000, L = 8, M = 128 * 128;\n  double *k = malloc(sizeof(double) * D * J);\n  double complex *f = malloc(sizeof(double complex) * M * L), *fhat = malloc(sizeof(double complex) * J * L),\n                 *ref = malloc(sizeof(double complex) * J);\n  srand(1);\n  for (int64_t i = 0; i < D * J; i++) k[i] = rand() / (RAND_MAX + 1.0) - 0.5;\n  for (int64_t i = 0; i < M * L; i++) f[i] = rand() / (RAND_MAX + 1.0) - 0.5;\n\n  /* L1 = 1 (nothing before the transformed dimensions), L3 = L (the images) */\n  int64_t p = nfftjl_plan_create_many(D, N, J, k, 1, L, 1e-8, 0);\n  nfftjl_trafo(p, f, fhat);                   /* fhat: J x L, image l at fhat + J*l */\n\n  int64_t q = nfftjl_plan_create(D, N, J, k, 1e-8, 0);\n  double e = 0, s = 0;\n  for (int64_t l = 0; l < L; l++) {\n    nfftjl_trafo(q, f + M * l, ref);\n    for (int64_t j = 0; j < J; j++) { e += pow(cabs(fhat[J * l + j] - ref[j]), 2); s += pow(cabs(ref[j]), 2); }\n  }\n  printf(\"difference to single plans %.1e\\n\", sqrt(e / s));\n\n  nfftjl_plan_destroy(p); nfftjl_plan_destroy(q);\n  free(k); free(f); free(fhat); free(ref);\n  return sqrt(e / s) < 1e-12 ? 0 : 1;\n}","title":"Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"c/#Threads","page":"C","text":"The Julia runtime starts on the first call into the library. Make that call from one thread before other threads call (nfftjl_num_threads() will do).\nPlans can be created and destroyed from any thread. One plan must not be executed by two threads at the same time.\nSeparate plans can be executed by several threads at the same time, each with its own nthreads.\nThe default number of threads of a plan (nthreads = 0) is NFFTJL_NUM_THREADS if set, otherwise the number of performance cores on Apple Silicon and of all CPU threads elsewhere. A 1D transform runs its FFT in one piece per thread, so a thread on an efficiency core or on a core busy with other work holds it up (up to 2× slower with all 10 threads of an M1 Max); on a busy machine fewer threads than cores are faster.","title":"Threads"}]
}
